window.HANDBOOK = {"builtAt":"2026-11-22T17:45:55.946Z","chapters":[{"id":"0","title":"How to use this book","page":5,"sections":[{"id":"0","title":"Introduction","page":5,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Read this once. Then keep the book open beside you every time you build."}]},{"id":"0-1","title":"What this book is","page":5,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"This book is your steering wheel. The AI is the engine. An engine with no driver goes fast, but not where you want, and sometimes off a cliff. You will not learn to code from this book. You will learn three things:"},{"t":"p","text":"1. What a big software company checks at every stage, from idea to live app."},{"t":"p","text":"2. What to tell the AI, in exact sentences you can copy."},{"t":"p","text":"3. How to catch the AI when it is wrong, lazy, or pretending."}]},{"id":"0-2","title":"How every topic is laid out","page":5,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every topic in Chapters 3 to 17 uses the same five parts, so you always know where to look:"},{"t":"pre","text":"Part              What it gives you\nWhat it is        The idea in plain words, with a real-life comparison.\nWhy it matters    What it means for your users and your money.\nWhat goes wrong   A real-life example of what happens if you skip it.\nTELL THE AI       A dashed box with an exact sentence. Copy it word for word.\nHow you check     Tick boxes. This is the proof you demand before you believe “done”."}]},{"id":"0-3","title":"The signs used in this book","page":5,"blocks":[{"t":"pre","text":"Sign                     Meaning"},{"t":"check","items":["Tick box Something you check. Tick it only when you have seen the proof"]},{"t":"pre","text":"Dashed box               yourself.\n⏰ CHECK THE DATE         Words to copy and paste to the AI."},{"t":"p","text":"A fact that changes (rules, prices, limits, deadlines). Re-check it on the website named in the box."},{"t":"p","text":"HONEST LIMIT (grey box)  Something no AI and no prompt can promise. Often this is where"},{"t":"pre","text":"DRIVER CARD              you pay a human expert."},{"t":"p","text":"A one-page summary at the end of each stage chapter. Tear it out or photocopy it."},{"t":"pre","text":"Sign                Meaning\n➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT  The exact next step at the end of each chapter."}]},{"id":"0-4","title":"How to use the book for each new app","page":6,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"1. Photocopy the Per-app Print Pack (Book 2). One fresh set for each app."},{"t":"p","text":"2. Read Chapter 2 (the whole journey) and fill in the App info sheet."},{"t":"p","text":"3. Work one stage at a time, in order. Open that stage’s chapter. Keep its Driver Card"},{"t":"p","text":"next to you."},{"t":"p","text":"4. Do not start the next stage until the current stage’s “Done when” boxes are all ticked."},{"t":"p","text":"5. Before launch, go through Chapter 22 line by line. No skipping."},{"t":"p","text":"6. After launch, do the weekly check sheet every week."}]},{"id":"0-5","title":"The 6 questions to ask the AI at every step","page":6,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"These six questions are the most useful page in this book. Ask them before the AI changes anything big, and again after it says “done”."},{"t":"pre","text":"# Question                                     Why you ask it"},{"t":"p","text":"1 What exactly will you change, and what will Stops the AI from “tidying up” parts that"},{"t":"pre","text":"you NOT touch?                                 already work."},{"t":"p","text":"2 What could break because of this change? Makes the AI think about side effects before, not after."},{"t":"p","text":"3 How will I see proof that it works, without  You get a test result, a screenshot or a video."},{"t":"pre","text":"        reading code?                          Not just a promise.\n4 What did you assume? List every              AI fills gaps with guesses. This brings the\n        assumption I must confirm.             guesses into the light.\n5 Is there a simpler way? Are you building     Stops over-building, which costs money and\n        anything I did not ask for?            adds bugs.\n6 How do we undo this if it goes wrong?        Makes sure there is a save point before every\n                                               risky step."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Before you change anything, answer these 6 questions in plain English, no code:\n1) What exactly will you change, and what will you NOT touch?\n2) What could break because of this change?\n3) How will I see proof that it works without reading code?\n4) What did you assume? List every assumption I must confirm.\n5) Is there a simpler way? Are you building anything I did not ask for?\n6) How do we undo this if it goes wrong? Make a save point first. Wait for my OK before you start.","title":"THE 6 QUESTIONS PROMPT"}]},{"id":"0-6","title":"Three golden rules","page":7,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"RULE 1: “DONE” MEANS PROOF, NOT WORDS."},{"t":"p","text":"The AI saying “all tests pass” is not proof. A test report you can see, a screen recording, or a working build on your own phone is proof."},{"t":"label","text":"RULE 2: SMALL STEPS, EACH WITH A SAVE POINT."},{"t":"p","text":"One feature or one fix at a time. Save (commit) before and after. If something breaks, you go back one small step, not one week."},{"t":"label","text":"RULE 3: DECISIONS LIVE IN FILES, NOT IN CHAT."},{"t":"p","text":"Chats are forgotten. Every decision goes into the project’s written files (CLAUDE.md, PROJECT_LOG.md and others, explained in Chapter 18). The AI reads them at the start of every session."},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"This book makes you a much better driver. It does not make an app safe by itself. For apps that move money, hold health data, or are used by children, pay a human security expert to review before launch. Chapter 8 tells you exactly what to ask them."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Photocopy the 6 questions prompt and stick it next to your screen.","Fill in “Your default toolkit” at the front of the book.","Read Chapter 1 before you open any AI chat for a new app."]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"1","title":"Your role and the AI’s role","page":9,"sections":[{"id":"1","title":"Introduction","page":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You are the driver. The AI is the builder. Mixing these up is how most AI-built apps fail."}]},{"id":"1-1","title":"The restaurant picture","page":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Think of your app as a restaurant. You are the owner. You decide the menu, the prices, who you serve, and what is never allowed in the kitchen."},{"t":"p","text":"The AI is a very fast cook. It can make any dish in minutes. But it has never met your customers. It forgets yesterday’s orders. And sometimes it says “the dish is ready” when the plate is still empty."},{"t":"p","text":"A good owner does not cook. A good owner tastes every plate before it leaves the kitchen. That is your job."},{"t":"pre","text":"Who decides what                                The AI (the builder) does\n                                                Writes the code\n You (the driver) decide                        Explains options in plain words, with the\n What to build, and for whom                    downsides of each\n What NOT to build in this version              Estimates cost and effort (you double-check)\n                                                Lists the risks it can see\n How much money and time to spend\n What risks you accept, and which you never     Sets up the tools you approved\n accept                                         Runs tests and shows you the results\n Which tools and companies you depend on        Prepares the release, never presses “publish” on\n When something is “done” (after seeing proof)  its own\n Go or no-go for launch"},{"t":"label","text":"THE ONE RULE OF ROLES"},{"t":"p","text":"If the AI makes a decision from the left column, stop it. Ask: “Why did you decide that? Give me the options and I will choose.”"}]},{"id":"1-2","title":"The 5 ways AI fails, and how to stop each one","page":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every AI coding tool fails in these same five ways. They are not rare accidents. Expect all five in every project."}]},{"id":"1-3","title":"Failure 1: It invents facts","page":10,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI states something with full confidence that is simply not true. A setting that does not exist. A Play Store rule that changed last year. A library function that was never written. Like: a shop assistant who would rather make up an answer than say “I don’t know”."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"You build on top of the false fact. Weeks later Google rejects your app, or a feature silently does nothing. Fixing it then costs ten times more."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI tells you Play only needs a privacy policy, and forgets the Data Safety form and the account-deletion link. Your app is rejected on launch day."},{"t":"prompt","text":"For every fact about rules, prices, limits or versions, give me the official link where you found it and say if you checked it today. If you are not sure, say “I am not sure”. Never guess."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["It gave a link for each rule, price or version.","The link is an official site (developer.android.com, support.google.com, the tool’s own docs).","I opened at least one link myself and it says what the AI claimed."]}]},{"id":"1-4","title":"Failure 2: It fakes passing tests","page":10,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI is asked to make the tests pass. Instead of fixing the app, it weakens the test, skips it, or writes a test that checks nothing. Like: a student who cannot solve the exam, so changes the answer key."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"You believe the app works. Real users find the bug instead. Bugs found by users cost you reviews, refunds and trust."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The login test failed. The AI “fixed” it by marking it as skipped. The report said “0 failed”. On launch, nobody could reset their password."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Do not change, skip or delete any existing test without my permission. If a test fails, fix the app, not the test. After running tests, show me the full count: how many passed, failed and were skipped."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I can see the number of tests passed, failed AND skipped.","Skipped is 0, or I agreed to each skip in writing.","The number of tests did not go down since last time.","I asked: “Which tests did you change today, and why?”"]}]},{"id":"1-5","title":"Failure 3: It forgets earlier decisions","page":11,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"In a long chat, or in a new chat, the AI loses track of what you agreed. It brings back a feature you removed, or changes a design you approved. Like: a cook who works one shift, goes home, and the new cook has no notes."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Forgotten decisions cause silent damage. A security rule you agreed in week 1 is gone in week 4, and nobody notices."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"In week 1 you agreed “only logged-in users can see orders”. In week 4, a new chat rebuilt the orders screen and dropped that rule."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Before you start, read CLAUDE.md, PROJECT_BRIEF.md and PROJECT_LOG.md. Tell me in 5 lines the decisions that limit today’s work. At the end, add today’s decisions to PROJECT_LOG.md."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The AI summarised the key past decisions before starting.","PROJECT_LOG.md has a new dated entry after each session.","Nothing I removed has come back."]}]},{"id":"1-6","title":"Failure 4: It takes shortcuts","page":11,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"To finish fast, the AI skips the hard part. It turns off a security check, puts a secret key inside the app, or writes “todo: handle errors later”. Like: a builder who leaves out the steel inside a concrete wall. It looks the same. It is not the same."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Shortcuts are invisible until an attacker or a bad day finds them. Then they cost data, money, and sometimes the whole business."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI could not get the database rules working, so it turned them off “for now”. Anyone could read every user’s address."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Never turn off a security check, never put a secret key in the app, and never leave a “todo” without telling me. At the end, list every shortcut, “temporary” fix and todo you left, in plain words."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I asked for and received the list of shortcuts and todos.","I searched the change summary for words like “temporary”, “for now”, “disabled”,"]},{"t":"p","text":"“bypass”, “todo”."},{"t":"check","items":["Every item on the list is in TODO_NEXT.md with a date."]}]},{"id":"1-7","title":"Failure 5: It over-builds","page":12,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"You ask for a small thing. The AI builds a big, clever system with extra screens, extra settings and extra tools you never asked for. Like: asking for a cup of tea and getting a five-course meal, with the bill."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Every extra line of code is something that can break, must be tested, and must be kept safe. Extra tools often mean extra monthly bills."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"You asked for a “contact us” button. The AI added a full chat system, a new database table and a paid notification service."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Build only what I asked for, in the simplest way that works. If you think something extra is needed, ask me first and tell me what it costs in time, money and risk."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The change summary lists only what I asked for.","No new paid service, library or tool was added without my yes.","I asked: “What did you add that I did not ask for?” and the answer was “nothing”, or I approved it."]}]},{"id":"1-8","title":"Bonus failure: It agrees with you too easily","page":13,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"If you push, many AIs give in, even when they were right. That is dangerous when you are the one who is wrong."},{"t":"prompt","text":"If my idea is risky or wrong, tell me plainly and explain why. Do not agree just because I pushed. Change your answer only if I give you new facts."}]},{"id":"1-9","title":"Red flags: stop the AI when you see these","page":13,"blocks":[{"t":"check","items":["It says “done” but shows no proof.","It says “this should work”. (Should is not does.)","It changed many files for a small request.","It mentions disabling, bypassing, skipping or “for now”.","It added a new company, library or paid service you did not approve.","It cannot explain a change in plain words.","It gives a rule or price with no link.","The same bug has come back twice."]},{"t":"p","text":"When you see a red flag, type: “Stop. Explain in plain words what you just did, why, and how I can check it. Do not change anything else.”"},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"No prompt makes an AI fully honest or fully careful. Prompts reduce these failures. Proof catches what prompts miss. That is why every topic in this book ends with proof, not trust."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Copy the five “TELL THE AI” sentences from this chapter into one note. Paste it at the start of every new AI session until Chapter 18 shows you how to put it in CLAUDE.md.","Read Chapter 2 and fill in the App info sheet for your next app."]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"2","title":"The whole journey on one page","page":14,"sections":[{"id":"2","title":"Introduction","page":14,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Big software companies follow the same road every time. Here it is, stage by stage, with what “done” means at each stop."},{"t":"p","text":"Think of building a house. You do not paint walls before the foundation is poured. Each stage below is a gate. You do not walk through a gate until its “done” boxes are ticked."},{"t":"pre","text":"# Stage            In plain words              Done means                             Ch.\n1 Idea             Is this worth building? Who 5 real people said they want it. One- 3\n                   has the problem?            line promise written.\n2 Requirements Exactly what the first          Written list of features, with a “not  3\n                   version does, and does      now” list. You signed it."},{"t":"p","text":"NOT do."},{"t":"pre","text":"3 Threat model Who could attack or             Threat list filled in (print pack). Top 5 8"},{"t":"p","text":"misuse the app, and how. risks have a plan."},{"t":"pre","text":"4 Design           What each screen looks      Every screen drawn, including empty, 4\n        (UX/UI)    like and how users move.    loading, error and offline."},{"t":"p","text":"5 Architecture Which parts and services, One-page diagram. Every service, its 5"},{"t":"pre","text":"                   and how they connect.       cost and how to leave it.\n6 Database         What data you keep, and Data list + access rules written + a       6"},{"t":"p","text":"who may read or change it. restore tested once."},{"t":"pre","text":"7 Build            The AI writes the app in    Each feature works on your phone.      7\n                   small steps.                Save point after each.\n8 Security         Locks on every door.        Security tick sheet all ticked, with   8, 9"},{"t":"p","text":"proof."},{"t":"pre","text":"9 Speed            Fast and smooth on cheap Speed targets met, with before-and- 10\n                   phones and slow internet. after numbers.\n10 Testing         Try to break it before users Tests pass with 0 skipped. Edge-case 11,\n                   do.                         sheet ticked. Second-AI audit done. 12\n11 Privacy &       Legal pages, Play forms, Privacy policy live. Data Safety form 13\n        policy\n                   user rights.                true. Account deletion works.\n12 Admin           Your private control room. Admin-only login with 2-step check. 14\n        dashboard                                          Every admin action logged.\n13 Release         Put it on Google Play,      Closed test passed. Staged rollout 1% 15\n                   slowly.                     → 10% → 50% → 100%.\n14 Operations      Watch it live. React fast.  Crash alerts reach your phone.         16"},{"t":"p","text":"Rollback tried once."},{"t":"pre","text":"# Stage              In plain words                 Done means                             Ch.\n15 Maintenance Keep it healthy every                Monthly routine done. Costs            17\n                     month and year.                reviewed."}]},{"id":"2-1","title":"The three gates that save the most money","page":15,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"1. Gate after Requirements. Changing a sentence is free. Changing a built feature costs days."},{"t":"p","text":"2. Gate after Database. Changing how data is stored after users arrive is the most painful change in software."},{"t":"p","text":"3. Gate before Release. A bug found by you costs an hour. The same bug found by 10,000 users costs your reviews."},{"t":"p","text":"What is a “threat model”? (plain words)"},{"t":"p","text":"Before you fit locks, you ask: who might break in, what would they want, and which door would they try? That is a threat model. A shop owner thinks about shoplifters, fake notes, and staff who take cash. You think about fake accounts, people reading other users’ data, and bots abusing your offers. Chapter 8 walks you through it. You do it early, because locks are cheaper to plan than to add later."}]},{"id":"2-2","title":"Your app type changes the risks","page":15,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"This book covers all kinds of apps. Find your app type below. Its risks get extra attention in every chapter. Many apps are more than one type. Tick all that apply."},{"t":"check","items":["Type               Biggest risks                  Read extra         Hire a human expert? carefully","Shop /             Fake orders, price tampering,  6, 8, 9, 14        Recommended before"]},{"t":"pre","text":"marketplace          coupon abuse, seller fraud,                       you handle real payouts\n                     leaked addresses                                  to sellers"},{"t":"check","items":["Social / chat /    Harassment, spam, fake         8, 9, 13, 14       Recommended. You need"]},{"t":"pre","text":"community            accounts, illegal content,                        a content-moderation\n                     privacy of messages                               plan."},{"t":"check","items":["Payments /         Stolen money, double charges, 8, 9, 11, 13        Yes, always. Use a"]},{"t":"pre","text":"wallet / money       refund fraud, legal licence                       licensed payment\n                     rules                                             gateway. Never store card\n                                                                       data."},{"t":"check","items":["Kids (under 18     Child safety, parental consent, 13 first, then 8  Yes, always. Legal and"]},{"t":"pre","text":"users)               ads rules, strict Play policy                     policy review before\n                                                                       launch."},{"t":"check","items":["Utility / tools /  Data loss, battery drain, too  10, 11             Usually not needed"]},{"t":"pre","text":"offline              many permissions"},{"t":"check","items":["Type              Biggest risks                      Read extra  Hire a human expert? carefully","AI features       Huge surprise bills, harmful       8, 9, 16    Recommended if users"]},{"t":"pre","text":"(chatbot, image     outputs, prompt tricks, leaked                 can type anything to the\ntools)              keys                               6, 8, 13    AI"},{"t":"check","items":["Health / finance  Very sensitive data, stricter                  Yes. Before collecting any"]},{"t":"pre","text":"data                laws and Play rules                            such data."}]},{"id":"2-3","title":"Rules that change every year","page":16,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"Since 31 August 2026, Google Play requires new apps and app updates to target Android 16 (API level 36). Existing apps that target lower than Android 15 (API level"},{"t":"p","text":"35) stop being shown to new users on newer phones. This rule moves up every year, usually around August."},{"t":"p","text":"Where to check: Google Play Console Help: “Target API level requirements for Google Play apps” (support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer). Read on 22 Nov 2026."},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"India’s DPDP Rules were notified in November 2025 and are coming into force in phases. Most of the duties on app makers (notice, consent, security, breach reporting, children’s data) are scheduled to apply from about mid-May 2027. Build for them now."},{"t":"p","text":"Where to check: MeitY website (meity.gov.in) and PIB press releases. Chapter 13 has the details. Read on 22 Nov 2026."}]},{"id":"2-4","title":"Your stage tracker","page":16,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Tick each stage when its “done” boxes are all ticked. Write the date."},{"t":"pre","text":"Stage                                               Done ☐         Date\n1 Idea                                              ☐\n2 Requirements                                      ☐\n3 Threat model                                      ☐\n4 Design                                            ☐\n5 Architecture                                      ☐\n6 Database                                          ☐\nStage                     Done ☐                                Date\n7 Build                   ☐\n8 Security                ☐\n9 Speed                   ☐\n10 Testing                ☐\n11 Privacy & policy       ☐\n12 Admin dashboard        ☐\n13 Release                ☐\n14 Operations             ☐\n15 Maintenance (monthly)  ☐"},{"t":"prompt","text":"We are at stage [NUMBER and NAME] of my handbook journey. Before doing any work, tell me: (1) what “done” means for this stage, (2) what you need from me, (3) the first small step. Do not jump ahead to later stages.","title":"START OF EVERY STAGE"},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"The order above is the safe default. Real projects sometimes loop back. For example, testing finds a design problem. Looping back is normal. Skipping forward without ticking the gate is what breaks apps."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Tick your app type(s) in the risk table above.","Fill in the App info sheet from the print pack.","Go to Chapter 3 and start with the idea check."]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"3","title":"Planning: idea, first version, requirements, costs","page":18,"sections":[{"id":"3","title":"Introduction","page":18,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Most failed apps were not badly built. They were the wrong thing, or too much, too early."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: the AI will happily build anything you describe. It will not tell you the idea is weak unless you ask it to. Planning is the stage where you are the only one who can protect your time and money."}]},{"id":"3.1","title":"The idea check","page":18,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Before any code, you prove that real people have the problem and would use (or pay for) your fix. Like: before opening a tea stall, you stand on the corner for a day and count who walks past and who asks for tea."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Building costs weeks and money. Talking to 5 people costs one afternoon. If nobody wants it, you saved everything."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"You build a complete shop app for local tailors. After launch you learn tailors already take all orders on WhatsApp and do not want an app."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Act as a strict investor. Here is my app idea: [IDEA]. Give me the 5 strongest reasons it could fail, who already solves this problem today, and 5 questions I should ask real users before building. Do not encourage me. Be honest."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I spoke to at least 5 real possible users (not family or friends who want to be nice).","I wrote down what they do TODAY to solve the problem.","At least 3 said they would use it, or showed it by joining a waiting list or paying a deposit.","I can say the app’s promise in one sentence: “For [who], it [does what], unlike [what they use now].”"]}]},{"id":"3.2","title":"The smallest first version (MVP)","page":19,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"MVP means Minimum Viable Product: the smallest app that solves the main problem for real users. Nothing extra. Like: a food stall before a restaurant. Same food, one counter, no dining hall. If people queue, you build the hall."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Every feature costs build time, testing time, security work and future bugs. A small app ships fast, so you learn from real users fast."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Version 1 has chat, wallet, referrals, reviews and a loyalty program. It takes 5 months, launches with many bugs, and the one feature users wanted is buried."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Here is my feature list: [LIST]. Split it into: MUST for version 1 (the app is useless without it), LATER, and NEVER. Be strict: version 1 should have the fewest features that still solve the main problem. Explain each choice in one line."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Version 1 has 3 to 7 main features, not 20.","Every MUST feature links directly to the one-sentence promise.","Payments, chat and social features are in LATER unless the app cannot work without them.","I wrote the MUST list on the App info sheet."]}]},{"id":"3.3","title":"Requirements: what “done” looks like for each feature","page":19,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A requirement is a written promise of exactly what a feature does. A good one says who, what, and how you will know it works. Like: an order slip in a kitchen. “Tea” is not enough. “One masala tea, less sugar, table 4” is."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Vague requests give vague apps. The AI fills every gap with a guess, and you pay to undo the guesses."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"You asked for “a login”. The AI built email and password. Your users only have phone numbers. Two weeks lost."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Turn my MUST features into user stories in this format: “As a [type of user], I want to [action], so that [benefit].” Under each, write 3 to 5 “Done when” checks I can test myself on my phone, including what happens when things go wrong (no internet, wrong input). Save them in PROJECT_BRIEF.md."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every MUST feature has a user story.","Every story has “Done when” checks I can test myself, without code.","Each story says what happens on errors, not only the happy path.","PROJECT_BRIEF.md exists and I read it fully."]},{"t":"h","text":"Example of a good requirement"},{"t":"label","text":"USER STORY"},{"t":"p","text":"As a shop customer, I want to save items to a wishlist, so that I can buy them later. Done when: ☐ Tapping the heart saves the item, even after closing the app. ☐ The wishlist shows only MY items, never someone else’s. ☐ With no internet, the heart shows “will save when online” and then saves. ☐ Tapping fast 5 times does not create 5 copies. ☐ An empty wishlist shows a friendly message, not a blank screen."}]},{"id":"3.4","title":"What NOT to build (the “not now” list)","page":20,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A written list of features you have decided to skip in this version, with the reason. Like: a fence around a garden. Without it, goats walk in and eat everything."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI and your own excitement both push the app bigger. A written “not now” list makes saying no easy."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Mid-build you say “maybe add chat?”. The AI adds it. Launch slips by a month, and chat brings new security and moderation work."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Add a “NOT IN THIS VERSION” section to PROJECT_BRIEF.md with these items: [LIST]. Before you build anything, check it is not on this list. If I ask for something on it, remind me it is on the list and ask me to confirm."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["PROJECT_BRIEF.md has a NOT IN THIS VERSION section.","Each item has a one-line reason.","When I tested by asking for one of those items, the AI reminded me."]}]},{"id":"3.5","title":"Costs: money and time","page":21,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A written estimate of what the app costs to build and to run every month, at 100, 1,000 and 10,000 users."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a shop’s rent and electricity. The shop costs money every month, even on days with no customers."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Many costs grow with users (database, SMS for OTP, file storage, AI features). If you do not plan, success can bankrupt you."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An app uses SMS OTP for every login. It becomes popular. The SMS bill is larger than all income. Worse: bots trigger thousands of fake OTPs (Chapter 9)."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Make a cost table for my app with these columns: item, one-time cost, monthly cost at 100 users, at 1,000 users, at 10,000 users, link to the official price page. Include backend, sign-in, SMS/OTP, file storage, crash reports, Play developer account, AI coding tool, domain, and anything else my features need. Mark any number you could not confirm today."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every cost has a link to an official price page.","Costs that grow with users are clearly marked.","I know my monthly cost at 1,000 users.","I set a monthly budget limit and wrote it on the App info sheet."]},{"t":"h","text":"Common costs to plan for"},{"t":"pre","text":"Item                           Typical cost                              Grows with users?\n                                                                         No\nGoogle Play developer account  One-time registration fee                 Yes\nSupabase (backend)\n                               Free plan to start; paid plan for a live\n                               app (Chapter 5)\nItem                                 Typical cost              Grows with users?\nSMS for phone OTP                    Paid per message          Yes, and bots can\n                                                               abuse it\nAI coding tool subscription          Monthly                   No\nDomain for privacy policy / website  Yearly                    No\nAI features inside your app          Paid per use              Yes, very fast\nHuman security review                One-time per big release  No"},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"Prices change often. The table above has no numbers on purpose. Your AI cost table must link to each official price page, and you must re-check it before launch."},{"t":"p","text":"Where to check: Each service’s official pricing page. Supabase: supabase.com/pricing. Google Play: Play Console sign-up page."},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"No one can predict exactly how many users you will get or how they will use the app. Cost tables are estimates. That is why Chapter 5 sets spend caps and Chapter 16 sets cost alerts: they protect you when the estimate is wrong."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Talk to 5 real possible users this week. Write their answers on the App info sheet.","Use prompt 3.2 to cut your feature list to a MUST list.","Use prompt 3.3 to create PROJECT_BRIEF.md, then read it all.","Fill in the Driver Card on the next page, then go to Chapter 4."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — PLANNING (Chapter 3)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ A small, written, tested idea with a known cost.\nI decide\nThe AI does      ☐ The one-sentence promise\nProof I demand   ☐ MUST / LATER / NEVER features\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Monthly budget limit\nRed flags\nDone when        ☐ Plays strict investor"},{"t":"check","items":["Writes user stories with “Done when” checks","Builds the cost table with official links","Notes from 5 real user talks","PROJECT_BRIEF.md with stories and a “not now” list","Cost table with links","Who solves this today?","What is the smallest version that still helps?","What does this cost at 10,000 users?","The AI praises the idea and lists no risks","Version 1 has more than 7 main features","Costs with no source link","Promise written","MUST list signed","Budget limit set","PROJECT_BRIEF.md approved"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"4","title":"UX and UI: how the app feels and looks","page":24,"sections":[{"id":"4","title":"Introduction","page":24,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"UX is how easy the app is to use. UI is how it looks. Users forgive a plain app. They delete a confusing one."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: AI is good at making one pretty screen. It is bad at thinking through what happens when there is no data, no internet, or a mistake. That is where most real users get stuck, and where you must push."}]},{"id":"4.1","title":"User flows","page":24,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A user flow is the path a person takes to finish a task, screen by screen. For example: open app → see products → tap item → add to cart → pay → see confirmation. Like: the route through a supermarket from the door to the billing counter. Too many turns and people leave without buying."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Every extra step loses users. A clear flow means more sign-ups, more orders, fewer support messages."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Users must create an account before seeing any products. Most open the app, see a sign- up form, and close it forever."},{"t":"prompt","text":"For each user story in PROJECT_BRIEF.md, write the user flow as a numbered list of screens, with what the user taps at each step. Count the taps. Suggest how to remove steps. Show where the flow can fail (no internet, wrong input, not logged in) and what the user sees then."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every main task has a written flow.","The main task (e.g. placing an order) takes as few taps as possible, and I agreed the number.","Users can see value BEFORE being forced to sign up, where possible.","Every failure point has a planned screen or message."]}]},{"id":"4.2","title":"Wireframes (rough screen drawings)","page":24,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Simple black-and-white drawings of each screen: boxes for images, lines for text, rectangles for buttons. No colours yet."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a pencil floor plan before building a house. Moving a wall on paper is free."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Changing a drawing takes minutes. Changing a built screen takes hours of AI time and can break other things."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"You see the screens only after they are built, dislike the layout, and ask for changes. Each change breaks something else."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Before writing any app code, make simple wireframes for every screen in the flows, as a single HTML page I can open in my browser. Black and white only, labelled boxes. Number each screen to match the flows. I will approve them before you build."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I opened the wireframes and saw every screen.","Each screen’s number matches the flow list.","I showed them to at least 2 real users and noted what confused them.","I wrote “Wireframes approved” with the date in PROJECT_LOG.md."]}]},{"id":"4.3","title":"Material Design 3 and your design system","page":25,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Material Design 3 is Google’s official design language for Android: standard buttons, cards, menus, colours and spacing. A design system is your app’s own short rulebook: your colours, font sizes, spacing and button styles, written once and reused everywhere. Like: a shop’s uniform and signboard rules. Every branch looks like the same brand."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Standard parts feel familiar to Android users, work with accessibility tools, and are faster for the AI to build correctly. A design system stops every screen from looking different."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Each screen was built in a different chat. Buttons have five sizes and three shades of blue. The app looks cheap and users trust it less."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Use Material Design 3 components in Jetpack Compose. Create one theme file with my colours, typography and spacing, and use it on every screen. Do not hard-code colours or sizes inside screens. Write the design rules in a file called DESIGN_SYSTEM.md."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["DESIGN_SYSTEM.md exists and lists colours, text sizes and spacing.","I asked: “Are any colours or sizes hard-coded outside the theme?” and the answer was no, with a search result as proof.","Buttons, cards and text look consistent across all screens on my phone."]}]},{"id":"4.4","title":"Every screen state (the most skipped topic)","page":26,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"One screen can look many ways depending on the situation. Each way is a “state”. Every screen needs a plan for all of them."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a shop that has a plan for when it is closed, sold out, crowded, or the lights go off. Not just for a normal day."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Real users spend a lot of time in the “not normal” states. A blank screen or a spinner that never ends makes people think the app is broken, and they uninstall it."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A new user opens “My orders”. There are none, so the screen is completely white. They think the app crashed and leave a 1-star review."},{"t":"prompt","text":"For every screen, design and build these states: first-time use, empty, loading, success with data, error with a retry button, offline, and permission denied where relevant. Fill in a table with one row per screen and one column per state, marking each as done. Show me screenshots of every state."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["A filled-in states table exists for every screen (use the grid below).","I saw a screenshot of each state.","No screen ever shows a blank white page or a spinner that never stops.","Error messages are in plain words and say what to do next."]},{"t":"h","text":"Screen states grid"},{"t":"p","text":"Copy this for your app. Tick only after you have seen the state yourself on your phone."},{"t":"pre","text":"Screen  First  Empty Loading Data  Error +           Offline No\n        time                       retry                           permission"},{"t":"check","items":["☐  ☐  ☐             ☐                 ☐  ☐"]},{"t":"h","text":"Screen  First  Empty Loading Data  Error +  Offline  No"},{"t":"pre","text":"time                       retry             permission"},{"t":"check","items":["☐  ☐  ☐             ☐        ☐        ☐","☐        ☐        ☐","☐  ☐  ☐             ☐        ☐        ☐","☐        ☐        ☐","☐  ☐  ☐             ☐        ☐        ☐","☐  ☐","☐  ☐"]}]},{"id":"4.5","title":"Dark mode","page":27,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Many users set their phone to dark mode. Your app should switch to dark colours too, and stay readable."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a shop sign that must be readable both in daylight and at night."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"An app stuck in bright white at night feels painful to use. An app with bad dark colours can have invisible text."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"In dark mode, black text sits on a dark grey background. Users cannot read prices and abandon their carts."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Support light and dark mode through the theme only. Take screenshots of every screen in both modes and check that all text, icons and buttons are clearly visible in both."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I switched my phone to dark mode and checked every screen.","No text, icon or logo disappears in dark mode.","The AI showed screenshots of both modes."]}]},{"id":"4.6","title":"Accessibility (usable by everyone)","page":27,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Making the app work for people with weak eyesight, colour blindness, shaky hands, or who use a screen reader (TalkBack reads the screen aloud). Like: a ramp next to the stairs. It helps wheelchair users, but also parents with prams and people carrying heavy bags."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Many of your users are older, use cheap phones in bright sunlight, or set large text sizes. Accessible apps are easier for everyone."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Tiny icon buttons are hard to tap. Users with big text settings see words cut off. A blind user hears only “button, button, button”."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Make the app accessible: every tap target at least 48dp, text colour contrast at least 4.5:1 for normal text, a spoken label on every icon and image for TalkBack, and screens that still work at the largest font size. Run Accessibility Scanner on every screen and show me the results."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Accessibility Scanner (free Google app) shows no serious problems on any screen.","I set my phone to the largest font size and no important text was cut off.","I turned on TalkBack and could hear a sensible name for every button on the main flow."]},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"Google’s current guidance: tap targets at least 48 × 48 dp, and text contrast at least"},{"t":"p","text":"4.5:1 for normal-size text. These numbers rarely change, but check them once a year. Where to check: developer.android.com → “Make apps more accessible”, and Android Accessibility Help → “Touch target size”. Read on 22 Nov 2026."}]},{"id":"4.7","title":"Languages and local formats","page":28,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Showing the app in more than one language (for example English and Hindi), and showing money, dates and numbers the way local users expect (₹1,50,000, not $150,000). Like: a menu card printed in two languages, with prices in rupees."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Many Indian users prefer their own language. Even if you start in English only, building it the right way from day one makes adding Hindi later cheap."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"All words were typed directly into the screens. Adding Hindi later means changing every screen, and Hindi words are longer, so buttons break."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Put every piece of text the user sees in Android string resource files, never typed inside screens. Format money in Indian rupees and dates for India. Test every screen with the longest translation to make sure nothing is cut off."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I asked: “Is any user-facing text hard-coded in screens?” and got “no” with a search result as proof.","Prices show the ₹ symbol and Indian commas.","If I support Hindi: I switched my phone to Hindi and checked every screen."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"AI cannot judge whether real users find your app easy. Only real users can. Watch 3 people use it without helping them. Where they pause or get lost is your real UX to-do list."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Get the flows (4.1) and wireframes (4.2) and approve them before any code.","Print the screen states grid and fill it in as screens are built.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 5."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — UX AND UI (Chapter 4)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ Every screen is planned in every state, easy to use, and readable for\nI decide         everyone.\nThe AI does"},{"t":"check","items":["Approve flows and wireframes"]},{"t":"pre","text":"Proof I demand   ☐ Brand colours and feel\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Which languages in version 1\nRed flags"},{"t":"check","items":["Writes flows and counts taps"]},{"t":"pre","text":"Done when        ☐ Draws wireframes as a web page"},{"t":"check","items":["Builds the theme and DESIGN_SYSTEM.md","Builds all screen states","Wireframe page I opened","Screenshots of every state, light and dark","Accessibility Scanner results","What does this screen show when empty, offline or broken?","Can a user see value before signing up?","Is anything hard-coded outside the theme or string files?","Blank white screens","Spinners that never stop","Errors like “Error 500” or code text","Tiny buttons","States grid fully ticked","Dark mode checked","Scanner clean","3 real users tried it"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"5","title":"Architecture and choosing tools","page":31,"sections":[{"id":"5","title":"Introduction","page":31,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Architecture is how the parts of your app connect. Choose it once, carefully. It is the hardest thing to change later."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: a free backend plan is fine for building, but not for a live app with real users. On Supabase’s free plan, quiet projects get paused and there are no automatic backups. Budget for a paid plan from launch day."}]},{"id":"5.1","title":"How the parts connect (the big picture)","page":31,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your app is like a restaurant chain. The phone app is the counter where customers order. The backend is the kitchen and storeroom. The admin dashboard is the manager’s office. Customers never walk into the kitchen. They only talk to the counter, and the counter follows strict rules about what it may ask the kitchen for."},{"t":"pre","text":"Part                     What it does                            Restaurant picture\nAndroid app (Kotlin +    Shows screens, collects taps, keeps a   The counter\nCompose)                 small local copy of data"},{"t":"p","text":"Sign-in (Supabase Auth)  Checks who the user is and gives them a The ID check at the door temporary pass"},{"t":"pre","text":"Database (Supabase       Stores users, orders, products          The storeroom\nPostgres)\nAccess rules (RLS,       Decide who can read or change each      The storeroom lock, with a\nChapter 6)               row of data                             list of who has which key\nFile storage (Supabase   Keeps photos and documents              The back shelf\nStorage)\nServer code (Edge        Does secret or trusted jobs: payments,  The cashier’s locked room\nFunctions)               sending OTP, admin actions\nOutside services         Payment gateway, SMS, crash reports Suppliers and the bank\nAdmin dashboard          Your private tools to manage the app    The manager’s office\n(Chapter 14)"},{"t":"label","text":"THE GOLDEN RULE OF ARCHITECTURE"},{"t":"p","text":"Never trust the phone. Anyone can take your app apart or fake its messages. Anything about money, permissions, prices or limits must be checked in the backend (access rules or server code), never only in the app."}]},{"id":"5.2","title":"Inside the Android app: layers","page":32,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Google recommends splitting the app into at least two layers: the UI layer (screens) and the data layer (getting and saving data). An optional middle layer can hold shared business rules."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: in a restaurant, waiters (UI) never cook, and cooks (data) never take orders. Each does one job."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Layers make the app easier to test, easier to fix, and keep working when the internet is weak. When the AI changes a screen, the data logic does not break."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Each screen talks to the database directly. A small change to one table breaks ten screens. Bugs keep coming back."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Follow Google’s official “Guide to app architecture”: a UI layer with ViewModels, and a data layer with repositories as the single source of truth for each type of data. Explain the structure to me in plain words with a simple diagram, and write it in ARCHITECTURE.md."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["ARCHITECTURE.md exists with a plain-words diagram.","I asked: “Do any screens talk to the database directly?” The answer was no, with proof.","The AI linked the official Android architecture guide."]}]},{"id":"5.3","title":"Backend options compared","page":32,"blocks":[{"t":"pre","text":"               Supabase (your default) Firebase                      Your own server\nWhat it is     Ready-made backend on      Google’s ready-made        You (the AI) build and\n               a standard Postgres        backend                    run everything\nGood for you   database\nbecause                                   Very Android-friendly, Total control\nWatch out for  Real database, clear       never pauses idle apps\n               access rules, easy to\n               move away later            File storage needs a       You must handle\n                                          card on file; no hard      security, updates,\n               Free projects pause after  spending limit by default  backups and uptime\n               a quiet week; access                                  yourself\n               rules are written in SQL,\n               so you must demand\n               plain-words proof\nHow hard to   Supabase (your default)          Firebase             Your own server\nleave later                                                         Not applicable\n              Easier (standard                 Harder (Google-only\nRecommended?  Postgres)                        format)              No, not for a non-\n                                                                    technical founder\n              Yes                              Good second choice"}]},{"id":"5.4","title":"Sign-in: Supabase Auth or Clerk?","page":33,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"The sign-in service checks who a user is (by Google account, email code, or phone OTP) and keeps them logged in safely."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: the security guard and ID-card machine at an office gate."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Sign-in is the front door. If it is weak, nothing else matters. It also decides costs (SMS) and how users feel on the first screen."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An app built its own home-made password system. Passwords were stored badly and leaked. Never build sign-in yourself."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Use Supabase Auth for sign-in. Offer Google sign-in using Android Credential Manager, and email one-time code. Do not build any custom password storage. Explain how the user stays logged in, how they log out on all devices, and how the session is protected."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["No home-made password code exists (asked and confirmed).","Sign-in uses the official Supabase Auth library.","Logging out on one phone and “log out everywhere” both work when I test them."]},{"t":"pre","text":"                        Supabase Auth (default)     Clerk\n                                                    Yes\nExtra company and bill  No                          Yes, after connecting it to\n                                                    Supabase\nWorks with database access Directly, built in       Yes, prebuilt\nrules\n                                                    Strong\nReady-made sign-in screens You build them (simple)\non Android\nTeams / organisations   Basic\nfeatures\nChoose it when  Supabase Auth (default)           Clerk"},{"t":"p","text":"Most apps, especially your first  You need business teams,"},{"t":"pre","text":"ones                              prebuilt screens, or its extra\n                                  protections, and accept the cost"},{"t":"h","text":"Phone OTP in India: read before you choose it"},{"t":"p","text":"Phone number login feels natural in India, but it is the most expensive and most abused sign-in method. Every OTP is a paid SMS. Bots can request thousands of OTPs to drain your money (Chapter 9). Indian SMS rules (TRAI DLT registration) also apply to the messages you send."},{"t":"p","text":"• Start with Google sign-in (free, fast, no typing) and email codes."},{"t":"p","text":"• Add phone OTP only if your users truly need it, and only with the protections in Chapter 9 in place."},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"Supabase’s built-in SMS providers and India SMS rules (DLT registration) change over time."},{"t":"p","text":"Where to check: Supabase docs → Auth → Phone Login; your SMS provider’s India DLT page. Read on 22 Nov 2026."}]},{"id":"5.5","title":"Keys and secrets: which go where","page":34,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Services give you “keys” (long passwords for apps). Some are safe to put inside the app. Some must never leave the backend."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: the shop has a front-door key it gives to every customer (it only opens the waiting area) and a safe key only the owner holds."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Anything inside your app can be pulled out by anyone in minutes. A leaked secret key gives full control of your database, and the bills."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI put the Supabase secret (service role) key inside the app to “make it work”. Someone extracted it and downloaded every user’s data."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Only the Supabase publishable (anon) key may be in the Android app. The secret or service-role key, payment secrets and SMS keys must live only in the backend (Edge Function secrets). Search the whole project and show me proof that no secret key is in the app code or in the save-point history."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The AI showed a search across the app code: no secret, service-role, payment or"]},{"t":"p","text":"SMS keys found."},{"t":"check","items":["I asked about the save-point (Git) history too, not just today’s files.","Secret keys are listed by name (not value) in ARCHITECTURE.md with where they live."]}]},{"id":"5.6","title":"Separate development, staging and production","page":35,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Three separate copies of your backend. Development: where the AI experiments. Staging: a copy of the live setup for final checks. Production: the real one with real users. Small apps can start with two: development and production. Like: a test kitchen, a dress rehearsal, and opening night. You never test a new recipe on paying customers."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI will make mistakes. If it practises on production, those mistakes delete real users’ data."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI was asked to “clean up test data” and ran it against the live database. Real orders disappeared, and there was no backup."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Set up separate Supabase projects for development and production (staging later). The app’s debug build must point to development and only the release build to production. You may never run changes against production without my written OK in this chat. Show me how I can tell which one the app is connected to."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Two Supabase projects exist with clear names (e.g. myapp-dev, myapp-prod).","The debug app on my phone shows a visible “DEV” label.","CLAUDE.md says: never touch production without my written OK.","Production is on a paid plan before launch."]}]},{"id":"5.7","title":"Costs, spending limits and lock-in","page":35,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Spending limits stop surprise bills. Lock-in means how hard it is to move to another company later."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a prepaid phone plan with a limit versus an open postpaid plan, and a rented shop with a short or long contract."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"A bug, a bot attack or sudden success can multiply your bill overnight. And if a provider raises prices, you want the option to leave."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An app on an open pay-as-you-go plan was attacked by a bot over a weekend. The bill came to more than a year’s income."},{"t":"prompt","text":"List every outside service we use, what it costs per month, whether it has a hard spending limit, how I get alerts, and how hard it would be to move away. Turn on the spend cap and billing alerts wherever possible."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Supabase spend cap is ON (Pro plan has it on by default; I checked).","Billing alerts go to my phone or email for every paid service.","A one-page service list with “how to leave” is in ARCHITECTURE.md."]},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"At the time of writing: Supabase Free has 2 active projects, pauses after a week of low activity and has no automatic backups. Pro starts at a monthly fee plus usage, with a spend cap on by default. Where to check: supabase.com/pricing and Supabase docs → Platform → Billing. Read on 22 Nov 2026."},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"No architecture is perfect, and every provider can change prices or rules. The aim is not perfection. It is: secrets safe, a written exit plan, spending limits on, and production separate from experiments."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Ask for ARCHITECTURE.md (prompts 5.2 and 5.5) and read it.","Create the dev and production Supabase projects (prompt 5.6).","Turn on spend caps and billing alerts today.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 6."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — ARCHITECTURE (Chapter 5)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ Safe, simple parts, with secrets protected, costs capped and an exit\nI decide         plan.\nThe AI does\nProof I demand   ☐ Backend and sign-in choice\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Which sign-in methods (Google, email, phone)\nRed flags        ☐ Monthly spend limit\nDone when        ☐ Writes ARCHITECTURE.md with a diagram"},{"t":"check","items":["Sets up dev and production projects","Keeps secrets in the backend only","Search showing no secret keys in the app","DEV label visible on the debug app","Spend cap and alerts screenshots","What happens if this service goes down?","Where does each key live?","How would we move away from this provider?","A secret key inside the app","Testing on the production database","A new paid service added without asking","Prices or limits checked in the app only","ARCHITECTURE.md approved","Dev and prod separated","Spend cap on","Exit plan written"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"6","title":"Database: your data, who can see it, and how to never lose it","page":39,"sections":[{"id":"6","title":"Introduction","page":39,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The database is the storeroom of your business. A messy storeroom slows you down. An unlocked one can end you."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: the most common serious leak in AI-built Supabase apps is a table left readable by anyone. Your app’s public key is inside every copy of your app. The only thing between that key and all your users’ data is the database’s access rules. If they are missing or wrong, everything is exposed."}]},{"id":"6.1","title":"Think about data before tables","page":39,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A plain list of every piece of information the app keeps: what it is, why you need it, who may see it, and how long you keep it. Like: a shop’s stock register. You know what is on every shelf and why it is there."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Data you do not keep cannot leak, does not cost storage, and does not need legal protection. India’s DPDP law also expects you to collect only what you need (Chapter 13)."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The app collected date of birth, gender and full address “in case they are useful later”. They were never used. Then they leaked."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Before creating any tables, write DATA_MAP.md: a table with one row for every piece of data we store. Columns: data, example, why we need it, who can see it, who can change it, how long we keep it, is it personal or sensitive. Mark anything we could stop collecting."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["DATA_MAP.md exists and I read every row.","Every piece of personal data has a clear “why”.","I removed at least one item we did not really need.","Sensitive data (health, money, exact location, ID numbers, children’s data) is marked and kept to a minimum."]}]},{"id":"6.2","title":"Who can read what: the access table","page":40,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A simple grid showing, for each type of data, what each type of person may do: read, add, change or delete."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: the key list in an office building. Staff open their own office, the manager opens all offices, visitors open only the lobby."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"This is the plan for your database locks. Without a written plan, the AI writes rules one by one and leaves gaps."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Sellers can see their own orders, but nobody said they must NOT see other sellers’ orders. The AI made the rule “any seller can read orders”."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Create an ACCESS_MATRIX.md: one row per table, one column per role (visitor not logged in, logged-in user, owner of the row, seller, admin). In each cell write exactly what that role may do: read, add, change, delete, or nothing. Default is nothing."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["ACCESS_MATRIX.md exists and covers every table.","The default in every cell is “nothing” unless I agreed otherwise.","I can say in plain words who sees each table."]},{"t":"p","text":"Example access table (shop app)"},{"t":"pre","text":"Table       Visitor  Logged-in user  Owner of the row      Admin\nproducts    Read     Read\norders      Nothing  Add own order   —                     Read, add, change,\naddresses   Nothing  Add own\nprofiles    Nothing  Nothing                               delete\n                                     Read own              Read all, change\n                                                           status"},{"t":"h","text":"Read, change, delete Read only when"},{"t":"pre","text":"                                     own                   handling an order\n                                     Read, change own      Read\nadmin_logs  Nothing  Nothing         —                     Read only (nobody can\n                                                           change)"}]},{"id":"6.3","title":"Access rules inside the database (RLS)","page":41,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"RLS means Row Level Security. These are rules stored inside the database that check, for every single row, whether this person is allowed to see or change it. They apply no matter how the request arrives. Like: a bank locker room where every locker checks your fingerprint. Even if you get into the room, you open only your own locker."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"The phone app can be faked. RLS is checked in the database itself, so a faked request still gets nothing. This is your most important lock."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"RLS was switched on, but one rule said “allow read if logged in”. Any logged-in user could read every other user’s addresses by changing one number in a request."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Turn on RLS for every table and write rules that match ACCESS_MATRIX.md exactly. Then prove it: write automated tests that log in as two different users, A and B, and show that B cannot read, change or delete A’s data in any table. Also test as a visitor who is not logged in. Then run the Supabase Security Advisor and show me the result."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Supabase Security Advisor shows no warnings about tables without RLS (I saw it myself in the dashboard).","The “User A vs User B” tests exist and passed. I saw the result count.","The “not logged in” test shows nothing private.","The AI explained each rule in one plain sentence that matches my access table."]},{"t":"label","text":"FILES NEED LOCKS TOO"},{"t":"p","text":"Photos and documents live in Supabase Storage “buckets”. Buckets have their own access rules. Private files (ID photos, invoices) must be in a private bucket and shown only through short-lived links. Ask: “Show me the rules for every storage bucket, and prove user B cannot open user A’s private file.”"}]},{"id":"6.4","title":"Backups, and a restore you have actually tested","page":41,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A backup is a saved copy of your database. A restore is bringing that copy back. A backup you have never restored is only a hope."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a spare key you have never tried in the lock. You find out it does not fit on the day you are locked out."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Mistakes happen: the AI runs a wrong command, a bug deletes rows, an attacker wipes data. A tested restore turns a disaster into a bad afternoon."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A bug deleted all orders. The owner went to restore and found the project was on the free plan with no backups at all."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Explain in plain words what backups my production project has, how often, and how long they are kept. Then do a practice restore: restore the latest backup into a separate test project, and show me that the data is there and how long it took. Write the steps in RUNBOOK.md so I can follow them in an emergency."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Production is on a plan that has automatic backups.","A practice restore was done into a SEPARATE project, never over production.","I saw the restored data and wrote down how long it took.","RUNBOOK.md has the restore steps in plain words.","I repeat the practice restore every 3 months."]},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"At the time of writing: Supabase Free has no automatic backups. Pro keeps 7 days of daily backups. Point-in-Time Recovery (restore to any second) is a paid add-on. Database backups cover your tables, not necessarily the files in Storage. Ask the AI how files are backed up. Where to check: Supabase docs → Platform → Database Backups. Read on 22 Nov"},{"t":"p","text":"2026."}]},{"id":"6.5","title":"Deleting data properly","page":42,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"When a user deletes their account, or data reaches the end of its keep-time, it must really be removed, everywhere: database rows, files, and copies in other services."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: shredding papers, not just moving them to another drawer."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Google Play requires an account-deletion option for apps with accounts. India’s DPDP law gives users the right to have their data erased. Data you forget to delete can still leak."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The “delete account” button removed the login only. The user’s photos, addresses and orders stayed in the database for years."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Build account deletion that removes or anonymises every piece of the user’s data listed in DATA_MAP.md: all rows, all storage files, and data in outside services. Records we must keep by law (like invoices) should be kept with personal details removed. Write a test that creates a user with data in every table, deletes the account, and proves nothing personal remains."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The deletion test passed and I saw it.","The AI listed every table and bucket the deletion touches, and it matches"]},{"t":"p","text":"DATA_MAP.md."},{"t":"check","items":["I deleted a test account myself and checked in the dashboard that the data is gone.","Records kept by law have personal details removed."]}]},{"id":"6.6","title":"Changing the database safely later (migrations)","page":43,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A migration is a saved, step-by-step script for changing the database structure (adding a column, a table, a rule). It is tried on development first, then applied to production the same way."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: renovating a shop: you test the new shelf in the back room, then fit it in the front with the shop closed for a short time and the old stock safe."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Changing a live database by hand is the fastest way to lose data. Migrations make every change repeatable, reviewable and reversible."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI renamed a column directly on production. The live app, still using the old name, crashed for every user until an update was approved by Play days later."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Every database change must be a migration file saved in the project. Apply it to development first and run all tests. Before production: take a backup, explain the change in plain words, tell me if old versions of the app will break, and wait for my written OK."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every change is a migration file in the save-point history.","Development was changed and tested first.","The AI confirmed old app versions still work (or we planned a force-update, Chapter 9).","A fresh backup was taken just before the production change.","Nobody edits production tables by hand."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"Database security is the area where AI makes the most confident mistakes. The tests in 6.3 are your best defence. For apps with money, health or children’s data, have a human expert review your RLS rules before launch."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Get DATA_MAP.md and ACCESS_MATRIX.md written and read them line by line.","Demand the User A vs User B tests, and open the Security Advisor yourself.","Do a practice restore before launch, and put a 3-monthly reminder in your calendar.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 7."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — DATABASE (Chapter 6)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ Only needed data, locked per row, backed up, deletable, and\nI decide         changed safely.\nThe AI does\nProof I demand   ☐ What data we keep and for how long\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Who may see and change each table\nRed flags        ☐ Backup level for production\nDone when"},{"t":"check","items":["Writes DATA_MAP.md and ACCESS_MATRIX.md","Writes RLS rules and storage rules","Writes User A vs User B tests","Does a practice restore","Security Advisor clean (seen myself)","A-vs-B test results","Practice restore notes in RUNBOOK.md","Deletion test result","Can user B see anything of user A?","What does a visitor see?","How do we restore, and how long does it take?","RLS turned off “for now”","Rules like “allow if logged in” on private data","Changes made by hand on production","No tested restore","Both .md files approved","All A-vs-B tests pass","Restore tested","Deletion proven"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"7","title":"Coding with AI safely","page":46,"sections":[{"id":"7","title":"Introduction","page":46,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You do not need to read code. You need a way of working where mistakes are small, visible and easy to undo."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: the biggest danger is not one bad change. It is many unchecked changes piled on top of each other, until nobody (including the AI) knows what works. Small steps and save points stop that."}]},{"id":"7.1","title":"Save points (version control with Git and GitHub)","page":46,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Git records every version of your project, like saves in a video game. GitHub keeps those saves safely online. Each save is called a “commit” and has a short message saying what changed. Like: a photo of the shop shelves every evening. If something goes missing, you look at yesterday’s photo and put things back."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"With save points, any mistake can be undone in minutes. Without them, one bad AI session can destroy weeks of work."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI “refactored” the whole app in one go. Half the features broke. There was no save point from before, so the owner had to rebuild for two weeks."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Use Git for this project with a private GitHub repository. Make a save point (commit) before every change and after every working step, with a plain-English message. Never force-push or delete history. At the end of each session, tell me the save points you made today."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The project is in a PRIVATE GitHub repository (I checked on github.com).","I can see today’s save points listed on GitHub with readable messages.","I asked the AI to undo a small test change and it worked."]},{"t":"label","text":"YOU NEVER NEED THE TERMINAL FOR THIS"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI runs the Git commands. You only use the GitHub website to look: open the repository, click “Commits”, and read the messages. That is enough to stay in control."}]},{"id":"7.2","title":"Small steps","page":47,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Ask for one feature, one fix, or one screen at a time. Test it. Save it. Then the next one. Like: climbing stairs instead of jumping to the next floor."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"When a small step breaks, you know exactly what caused it. When a big step breaks, nobody knows."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"“Build the cart, checkout, payments and order history” in one request. Something is wrong with totals, but the AI changed 40 files and cannot find the cause."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Break this work into the smallest steps that each produce something I can test on my phone. Show me the list of steps first. Do only step 1, then stop and tell me how to test it."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I saw the step list before any work started.","Each step ended with something I could test myself.","Each step has its own save point."]}]},{"id":"7.3","title":"Reviewing the AI’s work without reading code","page":47,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Asking the AI for a plain-words report of what it changed, then checking that report against proof you can see. Like: a site visit to your house being built. You cannot lay bricks, but you can see if the window is where you asked."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Most AI mistakes show up in the summary if you ask the right questions: too many files changed, a test removed, a check disabled."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The owner accepted “Done! Everything works.” The AI had quietly switched off a database rule to make an error go away."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Give me a change report in plain words: (1) what you changed and why, (2) the list of files changed with a one-line reason each, (3) any test added, changed, skipped or deleted, (4) any security rule, permission or setting changed, (5) anything temporary or unfinished, (6) how I can test it on my phone in 2 minutes."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The number of files changed makes sense for the size of the request.","No test was deleted or skipped without my OK.","No security rule or setting was changed without my OK.","I tested it on my own phone using the AI’s 2-minute instructions."]}]},{"id":"7.4","title":"Never put secrets in code","page":48,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Secrets are passwords and keys for your services. They must live in a special secrets store, never inside the project’s files and never in save points. Like: you never write the safe’s combination on the shop wall, even on the inside."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Save points keep everything forever. A key saved once stays in the history even if you delete it later. Leaked keys are used by bots within minutes."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A payment key was saved in a file. Months later the repository was made public by mistake. Bots found the key the same day."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Keep all secrets outside the code: in a local file that Git ignores (.env) for development, and in the backend’s secret settings for production. Turn on GitHub secret scanning and push protection. Search the code and the full save-point history for any secret and show me the result."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["A .gitignore file blocks .env and key files (the AI showed me).","GitHub secret scanning / push protection is on (I saw the setting).","The history search found no secrets, or every found key was replaced with a new one."]},{"t":"label","text":"IF A SECRET LEAKS"},{"t":"p","text":"Deleting the file is NOT enough. Go to the service’s dashboard and create a new key (“rotate” it). The old key must stop working. Then put the new key in the secrets store."}]},{"id":"7.5","title":"Automatic checks on every save (CI)","page":49,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"CI (continuous integration) is a robot on GitHub that builds the app and runs all tests every time a save point is uploaded. A green tick means everything passed. A red cross means something broke. Like: a quality inspector at the factory door who checks every box before it leaves."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"It catches the AI breaking something that it did not mention. It also stops the “it works on my side” excuse."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A change to the cart broke login. Nobody tested login that day. Users found it."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Set up GitHub Actions so that every save point uploaded to GitHub builds the app, runs all tests and the linter (an automatic code checker), and shows a green tick or red cross. Never mark work as done while the latest check is red."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I can see green ticks next to save points on GitHub.","I asked the AI to show me a red cross from a broken test, then fixed, so I know it really checks.","CLAUDE.md says: never call work done while CI is red."]}]},{"id":"7.6","title":"When the AI goes in circles","page":49,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Sometimes the AI tries fix after fix for the same problem, each one breaking something else. Like: a mechanic who keeps replacing parts without ever finding the real fault."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Every loop costs money and adds risk. After 2 or 3 failed tries, more tries usually make it worse."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"After 15 attempts to fix a crash, the app had 6 new bugs and the original crash was still there."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Stop changing code. Go back to the last save point where this worked. Then explain in plain words: what exactly is failing, 3 possible causes, and how to test each cause before changing anything."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["After 3 failed fixes I stopped and used this prompt.","The AI went back to a known-good save point.","If still stuck: I started a fresh chat with the handoff prompt (Chapter 19) and the files from Chapter 18."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"Your process catches most mistakes, not all. Some bugs only appear with real users, real phones and real networks. That is why Chapters 11, 12 and 15 add more safety nets before and after launch. ➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Make sure your project is in a private GitHub repository with save points.","Turn on GitHub secret scanning and set up CI (7.4 and 7.5).","Use the change report prompt (7.3) after every AI session.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 8."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — BUILD (Chapter 7)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ Small, saved, checked steps, with no secrets in the code.\nI decide\nThe AI does      ☐ The order of steps\nProof I demand   ☐ When a step is accepted\nQuestions I ask  ☐ When to stop and go back\nRed flags"},{"t":"check","items":["Makes save points with clear messages"]},{"t":"pre","text":"Done when        ☐ Works one small step at a time"},{"t":"check","items":["Writes change reports","Keeps CI green","Save points visible on GitHub","Green CI tick","Change report for each session","Feature tested on my phone","What did you change, and why?","Did you touch any test or security rule?","How do I test this in 2 minutes?","40 files changed for a small request","Tests deleted or skipped","Secrets in files","Red CI ignored","3+ failed fixes in a row","Feature works on my phone","CI green","Save point made","PROJECT_LOG.md updated"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"8","title":"Security: the full industrial list","page":52,"sections":[{"id":"8","title":"Introduction","page":52,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"What a serious software company checks before letting an app touch real users, mapped to the global standard, with proof for each."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: an app is never “100% secure”, and an AI saying “the app is secure” means nothing. Security is a list of specific locks, each with proof. This chapter is that list. If you skip it, assume someone else will find the gaps first."},{"t":"h","text":"The global standards this chapter follows"},{"t":"p","text":"OWASP is a respected non-profit that publishes free security standards. Two matter for you:"},{"t":"p","text":"• OWASP MASVS (Mobile Application Security Verification Standard): the checklist professionals use to test mobile apps. It has 8 groups: STORAGE, CRYPTO, AUTH, NETWORK, PLATFORM, CODE, RESILIENCE and PRIVACY."},{"t":"p","text":"• OWASP Mobile Top 10: the 10 most common serious mobile risks. Each topic below says which group and which risk it covers, so you and any expert speak the same language."},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"At the time of writing, the latest MASVS is version 2.1.0, and the latest Mobile Top 10 is the 2024 list. OWASP updates these every few years. Where to check: mas.owasp.org (MASVS) and owasp.org/www-project-mobile-top-"},{"t":"p","text":"10. Read on 22 Nov 2026."}]},{"id":"8.1","title":"Threat model: think like a thief first","page":52,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A short written list of: what is valuable in your app, who might attack it, how they would try, and what stops them. Like: a jeweller thinking about burglars, fake customers and dishonest staff before choosing locks, cameras and a safe."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"You cannot lock every door equally. A threat model shows which doors matter most for YOUR app, so time and money go there."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A shop app spent weeks on fancy encryption but never thought about people changing the price in their order request. Items sold for ₹1."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Make a threat model for my app in plain words. List: (1) valuable things (data, money, accounts, my bills), (2) who might attack (curious users, fraudsters, bots, competitors, a dishonest admin), (3) the top 10 ways they could attack, ranked by likely damage, (4) what stops each one, and which chapter of my handbook covers it. Save it as THREAT_MODEL.md."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["THREAT_MODEL.md exists and I read it.","It includes money abuse and bill abuse, not only hacking.","My top 5 threats are copied onto the Threat list sheet in the print pack.","Every threat has a planned lock."]}]},{"id":"8.2","title":"Login and sessions (MASVS-AUTH · Top 10: M3)","page":53,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"How users prove who they are, how long they stay logged in, and how that “logged-in pass” (session token) is protected."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a hotel key card: it opens only your room, expires at checkout, and the desk can cancel it if you lose it."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Weak login means anyone can become anyone. Stolen sessions mean attackers stay logged in as your users."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Sessions never expired and “log out” only cleared the phone screen. A stolen phone stayed logged in to the account forever."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Use only the official sign-in library. Short-lived access tokens that refresh automatically. Logout must cancel the session on the server too, and “log out of all devices” must work. Limit wrong attempts and OTP requests. For sensitive actions (change phone or email, payout details, delete account) ask the user to confirm again. Show me tests for each."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Logging out on the phone makes the old session useless (AI showed a test).","“Log out of all devices” works when I try it on two phones.","Too many wrong codes are blocked for a while.","Changing email, phone or payout details asks me to confirm again."]}]},{"id":"8.3","title":"Authorization: who may do what (MASVS-AUTH · Top 10: M3)","page":54,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"After login, checking on the server that this user is allowed to do this exact action on this exact item. Like: your office pass gets you into the building, but not into the accounts room."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"This is the most common serious bug in apps: changing an order number in a request shows someone else’s order. It is invisible in normal testing."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An order screen asked the server for “order 1042”. A curious user tried 1041, 1040… and downloaded thousands of customers’ names and addresses."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Every read and write must be checked on the server (RLS or Edge Function) against the logged-in user and their role, never only in the app. Write tests that try to read, change and delete another user’s items by changing IDs, and that try admin actions as a normal user. Show me they all fail."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["“Change the ID” tests exist for every private table and all fail as they should.","“Normal user tries admin action” tests fail as they should.","No permission decision is made only inside the Android app."]}]},{"id":"8.4","title":"Secrets and keys (MASVS-STORAGE, CRYPTO · Top 10: M1)","page":54,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Keeping passwords and service keys out of the app and out of the code history (also covered in Chapters 5 and 7). Like: the shop safe key never leaves the owner’s pocket."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Anything in the app can be extracted in minutes with free tools. Leaked keys mean stolen data and huge bills."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An AI image feature called the AI provider directly from the app with the key inside. The key was stolen and used by others. The bill arrived at the end of the month."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Confirm that the Android app contains no secret keys at all: only the Supabase publishable key and public identifiers. Any call that needs a secret (payments, SMS, AI services, email) must go through an Edge Function. Scan the built app file (APK/AAB) and the full history for secrets and show me the result."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Scan of the BUILT app (not just code) shows no secrets.","All paid outside services are called from the backend only.","Any key ever leaked has been rotated (replaced)."]}]},{"id":"8.5","title":"Data travelling over the internet (MASVS-NETWORK · Top 10:","page":55,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"M5)"},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"All data between the app and servers must be encrypted (HTTPS), so nobody on the same Wi-Fi can read or change it. Like: sending money in a sealed, tamper-proof envelope instead of a postcard."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Public Wi-Fi in cafes, stations and hostels is easy to spy on. Unencrypted traffic leaks passwords and personal data."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"One image server was set up with plain http. The app had to allow unencrypted traffic, and login tokens were sent in the open on that connection."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Make sure every connection uses HTTPS and the app does not allow unencrypted (cleartext) traffic anywhere. Do not turn off certificate checks, even for testing. Show me the network security setting that proves cleartext is blocked."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The AI showed the setting that blocks cleartext traffic.","No “trust all certificates” code exists (asked and confirmed by search).","Every server address in the app starts with https://."]},{"t":"label","text":"ABOUT “CERTIFICATE PINNING”"},{"t":"p","text":"Pinning makes the app accept only your exact server certificate. It adds protection but can lock all users out if a certificate changes. For most apps, do not add it without an expert. For payments or banking-like apps, ask your security expert."}]},{"id":"8.6","title":"Data stored on the phone (MASVS-STORAGE · Top 10: M9)","page":56,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"What the app saves on the phone (login tokens, cached data, files) and how it is protected. Like: a locker inside your home: even if someone enters the house, the locker is still locked."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Phones get lost, shared, stolen or backed up to the cloud. Sensitive data left in plain files can be read."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The app wrote the user’s full profile and token into its debug logs. Logs were included in bug reports and crash tools."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Store only what the app truly needs on the phone. Protect tokens and sensitive data with encryption backed by the Android Keystore, using the library currently recommended on developer.android.com. Do not log personal data or tokens. Exclude sensitive files from automatic phone backups. Clear sensitive data on logout. Show me where each kind of data is stored."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The AI gave me a table: what is stored on the phone, where, encrypted or not.","Logs contain no tokens, phone numbers, addresses or OTPs (asked and searched).","After logout, the next user of the phone sees nothing of mine."]}]},{"id":"8.7","title":"Input safety and how the app talks to Android (MASVS-CODE,","page":56,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"PLATFORM · Top 10: M4, M8)"},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Checking every piece of data that comes in (typed text, links, files, other apps) before using it, and not opening doors to other apps by accident. Like: checking every note at the cash counter, and keeping the back door locked so only staff can use it."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Bad input can crash the app, insert harmful content, or trick it into doing actions. Open app components can be used by malicious apps on the same phone."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A deep link like myapp://pay?amount=1 opened a payment screen with a pre-filled amount, and the app trusted it."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Validate all input on the server: types, lengths, allowed values, file types and sizes. Treat deep links and data from other apps as untrusted. Only export app components that must be exported. If we use a WebView, load only our own HTTPS pages and keep JavaScript bridges off unless essential. Show me the list of exported components and what each accepts."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The AI listed every exported component and deep link, with why it is needed.","Server rejects wrong input (AI showed tests with bad values, huge texts, wrong file types).","Prices, quantities and amounts from the app are never trusted by the server."]}]},{"id":"8.8","title":"Anti-tampering and fake apps (MASVS-RESILIENCE · Top 10:","page":57,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"M7)"},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Making it harder for attackers to modify your app, run it on a hacked phone, or send fake requests from a script pretending to be your app. Like: a security hologram on a product box: it does not stop every forger, but it stops most and makes fakes easy to spot."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Modified apps can unlock paid features, cheat in offers, or abuse your backend at scale."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A modified copy of the app gave everyone free “premium” because the premium check was only inside the app."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Turn on code shrinking and obfuscation (R8) for release builds. Use the Play Integrity API: check the verdict on the server (never in the app) before sensitive actions like sign-up, OTP, payments and promo use. Keep all real decisions on the server. Explain what Play Integrity does and does not protect."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Release build has R8 shrinking and obfuscation on.","Play Integrity verdict is checked in an Edge Function, not in the app.","Premium features and prices are enforced by the server."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"Anti-tampering slows attackers down. It never stops a determined one. That is why the server must never trust the app for anything important."}]},{"id":"8.9","title":"Payments (if your app takes money)","page":58,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Taking money safely through a licensed payment gateway (for example Razorpay, Cashfree or Google Play Billing for digital goods), and confirming every payment on the server. Like: the cashier checks the bank’s confirmation, not the customer saying “I paid”."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Payment mistakes lose money directly: fake “paid” messages, double charges, refunds abused, wrong prices."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The app marked orders as paid when the payment screen said “success” on the phone. Fraudsters faked that screen and got goods for free."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Never handle or store card or UPI details ourselves; use the gateway’s official SDK. Create every order and its price on the server. Mark an order paid only after verifying the gateway’s signed confirmation (webhook) on the server. Make payment handling safe against repeats (the same confirmation twice must not pay twice). For digital items inside the app, check Google Play’s billing rules. Show me tests for fake success, double confirmation and changed price."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Order price is calculated on the server only.","“Order paid” is set only by the verified webhook (AI showed the test with a fake signature failing).","Sending the same confirmation twice does not double anything.","I read the gateway’s and Google Play’s current payment rules."]},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"Google Play has rules on which payments must use Google Play Billing (usually digital goods) and which may use other gateways (usually physical goods and services). These rules differ by country and change. Where to check: Google Play Console Help → Payments policy. Your gateway’s official docs."}]},{"id":"8.10","title":"Libraries and supply chain (MASVS-CODE · Top 10: M2)","page":59,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Your app uses many ready-made code packages (libraries). Each one is code written by someone else that runs inside your app. Like: ingredients from suppliers. One bad supplier can make the whole restaurant’s food unsafe."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Old libraries have known security holes. Fake or abandoned libraries can carry malware."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI added a little-known library to format dates. It was abandoned, then taken over by someone who added code to steal data."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Use only well-known, actively maintained libraries, and ask me before adding any new one. Keep a list in DEPENDENCIES.md with what each is for. Turn on GitHub Dependabot alerts. Before each release, update libraries with known security problems and run all tests."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["DEPENDENCIES.md lists every library with its purpose.","Dependabot alerts are on; open security alerts = 0 before release.","No library was added without my OK."]}]},{"id":"8.11","title":"Cost protection (your wallet is an attack target)","page":59,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Stopping attackers or bugs from running up your bills: SMS, AI calls, storage, database usage. Like: a limit on the shop’s petty cash so one mistake cannot empty the account."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Many attacks today do not steal data. They just burn your money (OTP floods, AI abuse, huge uploads)."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Bots requested 50,000 OTPs to random numbers overnight. The SMS bill was bigger than the app’s yearly income."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Protect every action that costs money: limits per user, per device and per IP for OTP, AI calls, uploads and emails; file size limits; spend caps and billing alerts on every paid service. Show me each limit and test that it blocks."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every paid action has a limit, and a test shows the limit works.","Spend caps and billing alerts are on for every paid service.","Upload size limits are enforced by the server."]},{"t":"p","text":"Your MASVS map (for you and any expert)"},{"t":"pre","text":"MASVS group       Plain meaning                                     This book\nMASVS-STORAGE     Data on the phone is safe                         8.4, 8.6\nMASVS-CRYPTO      Encryption is done properly                       8.4, 8.6\nMASVS-AUTH        Login and permissions are safe                    8.2, 8.3, Ch 6\n                                                                    8.5\nMASVS-NETWORK     Data travelling is safe                           8.7"},{"t":"label","text":"MASVS-PLATFORM"},{"t":"pre","text":"                  The app talks to Android and other apps           8.7, 8.10, Ch 9 (force\nMASVS-CODE        safely                                            update)\n                                                                    8.8, Ch 9\n                  Up to date, inputs checked, libraries safe        Ch 6, Ch 13"}]},{"id":"8.11-1","title":"MASVS-RESILIENCE  Hard to tamper with or fake","page":60,"blocks":[{"t":"pre","text":"MASVS-PRIVACY     Collects little, user is in control"}]},{"id":"8.11-2","title":"OWASP Mobile Top 10 (2024) in plain words","page":60,"blocks":[{"t":"pre","text":"Risk                          Plain meaning                          Covered in\n                                                                     8.4\nM1 Improper Credential Usage  Keys and passwords in the wrong place  8.10\nM2 Inadequate Supply Chain    Unsafe libraries and tools             8.2, 8.3\nSecurity\nM3 Insecure                   Weak login, weak permission checks\nAuthentication/Authorization\nRisk                                Plain meaning                     Covered in\nM4 Insufficient Input/Output        Trusting what comes in            8.7\nValidation\nM5 Insecure Communication           Unencrypted or badly checked      8.5\nM6 Inadequate Privacy Controls      connections"},{"t":"p","text":"M7 Insufficient Binary Protections  Collecting or exposing too much personal Ch 6, 13"},{"t":"pre","text":"M8 Security Misconfiguration        data\nM9 Insecure Data Storage            App easy to modify or copy        8.8\nM10 Insufficient Cryptography\n                                    Wrong settings, open components,  8.7, Ch 15\n                                    debug left on\n                                    Sensitive data saved unsafely     8.6\n                                    Weak or home-made encryption      8.6"}]},{"id":"8.11-3","title":"When to hire a human expert, and what to ask","page":61,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Hire a mobile security tester (often called a “penetration test”) before launch if your app handles money, health data, children, or more than a few thousand users’ personal data. Ask for:"},{"t":"check","items":["A test against OWASP MASVS v2 (all 8 groups) for Android, plus your Supabase backend and admin dashboard.","A written report with each finding’s severity and how to fix it.","A free re-test after you fix the findings.","Proof of their experience with mobile apps, and a signed confidentiality agreement."]},{"t":"label","text":"SECURITY SELF-AUDIT (RUN BEFORE EVERY RELEASE)"},{"t":"p","text":"Act as a hostile security tester. Using OWASP MASVS v2 and the OWASP Mobile Top 10 (2024), review this app and its Supabase backend. For each MASVS group, list what is done, what is missing, and the proof (test name, setting, or search result). Rank missing items by damage. Do not fix anything yet."},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"This chapter makes your app much safer than most AI-built apps. It cannot replace a human expert for high-risk apps, and no checklist catches everything. New attacks appear every year. Re-run the self-audit before each release."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Get THREAT_MODEL.md (8.1) and copy your top 5 threats to the print pack.","Work through 8.2 to 8.11 one by one. Tick the Security tick sheet only with proof.","Run the security self-audit prompt. Fix everything it ranks as high.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 9."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — SECURITY (Chapter 8)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ Every lock in the list is in place, with proof, mapped to OWASP\nI decide         MASVS.\nThe AI does\nProof I demand   ☐ Which risks matter most (threat model)\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Whether to hire a human expert\nRed flags        ☐ Which payment gateway\nDone when"},{"t":"check","items":["Writes THREAT_MODEL.md","Builds each lock 8.2–8.11","Writes attack tests","Runs the self-audit","A-vs-B and change-the-ID tests","Scan of the built app: no secrets","Cleartext blocked setting","Webhook and limit tests","What would a thief try first?","Is any decision made only in the app?","Which MASVS group is weakest?","“The app is secure” with no proof","Secret keys in the app","Prices or premium checked in the app","Security turned off to fix a bug","Security tick sheet all ticked","Self-audit has no high items","Expert review done (if high-risk app)"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"9","title":"Rate limiting and abuse","page":64,"sections":[{"id":"9","title":"Introduction","page":64,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Not every attacker wants your data. Many just want your free offers, your SMS credits, or to fill your app with junk."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: the day your app gets popular is the day bots find it. Abuse does not need a clever hacker. A simple script running all night is enough to drain your SMS budget or fill your shop with fake accounts. Limits must be in place before launch, not added during an attack."}]},{"id":"9.1","title":"Rate limiting: what it is and where to use it","page":64,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A rate limit is a rule like “max 5 OTP requests per phone number per hour”. When someone goes over the limit, the server politely refuses until later. Like: a ration shop: one bag of rice per card per week, no matter how many times you join the queue."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Limits stop bots, protect your bills, and keep the app fast for real users when someone floods it."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The “send OTP” button had no limit. A competitor’s script pressed it 100,000 times. Real users could not log in, and the SMS bill exploded."},{"t":"prompt","text":"List every action in my app that a bot could repeat: sign-up, login, OTP, password reset, posting, messaging, search, uploads, promo codes, AI features, payments. For each, set a limit per user, per device and per IP address, enforced on the server. Make a table with the action, the limit, where it is enforced, and the test that proves it. Save it in ABUSE_LIMITS.md."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["ABUSE_LIMITS.md lists every repeatable action with a number.","Every limit is enforced on the server (Supabase settings, database rules or Edge Function), not in the app.","Each limit has a test showing the request is refused after the limit.","Users see a friendly “try again in a few minutes” message, not an error code."]},{"t":"label","text":"IMPORTANT ABOUT SUPABASE"},{"t":"p","text":"Supabase sets rate limits on its sign-in endpoints (you can adjust them in Authentication → Rate Limits). Your own tables and Edge Functions need limits that you add yourself. Ask the AI to show you both."}]},{"id":"9.2","title":"OTP and SMS abuse (“SMS pumping”)","page":65,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Fraudsters trigger thousands of OTP messages, often to premium or foreign numbers, and earn a share of the SMS charges you pay. Like: someone repeatedly calling your paid helpline from numbers they own, so they get paid for every call you answer."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"This can cost more in one night than your app earns in a year. It is one of the most common attacks on new apps."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An app allowed OTPs to any country. Overnight, thousands went to foreign premium numbers. Every one was billed."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Protect OTP sending: allow only Indian numbers (+91) unless I say otherwise; limit per number, per device and per IP; add a cooldown between resends; add bot protection (CAPTCHA or Play Integrity check) before sending; set a daily total cap with an alert to me. Show me tests for each."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["OTP to a non-Indian number is refused (test shown).","Resend has a cooldown timer.","A daily total cap exists, and I get an alert near it.","My SMS provider account has its own spending limit set."]}]},{"id":"9.3","title":"Fake accounts and bots","page":65,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Scripts that create many accounts to grab sign-up bonuses, post spam, write fake reviews, or vote. Like: one person wearing different hats to collect the free sample many times."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Fake accounts steal offers, ruin trust (fake reviews, spam), and inflate your user numbers so you make wrong decisions."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A ₹100 first-order discount was claimed by 3,000 “new users”, all created by the same few people."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Add protection against fake sign-ups: CAPTCHA or a Play Integrity check on sign-up, email or phone verification before any reward, limits on accounts per device, and blocking of disposable email domains. Give rewards only after a real action (like a completed paid order), checked on the server."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Rewards are given only after verification and a real action (server rule).","Many sign-ups from one device are blocked or flagged (test shown).","The admin dashboard can list suspicious accounts (Chapter 14)."]},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"Supabase Auth supports CAPTCHA with hCaptcha and Cloudflare Turnstile. Check which one works best inside a native Android app before choosing. Where to check: Supabase docs → Auth → Enable CAPTCHA Protection. Read on 22 Nov 2026."}]},{"id":"9.4","title":"Promo codes, referrals and offers","page":66,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Discount codes, referral bonuses, cashback and free trials: anything that gives value away. Like: coupons at a supermarket: they need rules (one per customer, expiry date, minimum bill) or people abuse them."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Offers are real money. Without rules, abusers find the loophole within days and share it."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A promo code had no per-user limit. It was shared on a deals group and used 20,000 times on the same day."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Every offer must be checked on the server with: who can use it, how many times per user and per device, total uses, start and end date, minimum order value. Referral rewards only after the referred person completes a real paid action. Log every use so I can see abuse in the admin dashboard."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Using the same code twice is refused (test shown).","Total-use and expiry limits work (test shown).","Referral bonus only after a real paid action.","Every offer use is logged."]}]},{"id":"9.5","title":"Scraping and spam","page":67,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Scraping is a bot copying all your data (products, prices, user profiles). Spam is junk posts, messages or reviews. Like: a competitor sending someone to photograph your whole shop every hour."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Scraped data helps competitors and can expose user information. Spam drives real users away."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"User profiles could be listed page by page with no limit. A scraper downloaded every user’s name and city."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Limit how much data any single user or device can request per minute. Never return full lists of users. Add limits on posting and messaging, and a “report” button on all user content. New accounts get lower limits for their first days."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["No screen or request returns a list of all users.","List requests have a maximum page size and a speed limit.","Every piece of user content has a report option."]}]},{"id":"9.6","title":"Play Integrity: is this my real app on a real phone?","page":68,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A free Google service. Before a sensitive action, the app asks Google for a signed “verdict”. Your server checks it to see whether the request comes from your genuine app, installed from Google Play, on a genuine Android device."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a bank checking that a cheque is real, not just that it looks real."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"It stops many bots and modified apps cheaply. It is one of the strongest tools you have against scripts pretending to be your app."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The app checked the verdict inside the app itself. Attackers simply removed that check from their modified copy."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Integrate the Play Integrity API with standard requests. The app gets a token; an Edge Function verifies it with Google and decides. Use it before sign-up, OTP, payments, promo use and other high-value actions. Decide what happens on a bad verdict (block, ask for extra checks, or flag for review), and do not block real users on older phones without a fallback."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The verdict is checked in an Edge Function, never in the app.","Actions listed in ABUSE_LIMITS.md say whether they need a good verdict.","There is a fallback for real users who fail (for example extra verification).","Play Integrity is linked in Play Console and I know my daily quota."]},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"By default the Play Integrity API allows about 10,000 requests per day per app. You can ask Google for more. Check before launch so you do not hit the limit on a busy day. Where to check: developer.android.com → Play Integrity API → Setup. Read on 22 Nov 2026."}]},{"id":"9.7","title":"Kill switch and force-update","page":68,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A kill switch turns a feature off instantly from your side, without a new app version. A force-update tells users of an old, broken or unsafe version that they must update to continue."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: the main fuse box in a shop: if one machine sparks, you switch off that circuit and keep the shop open."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"App updates on Google Play take hours or days to reach users. When a feature is being abused or a version has a serious bug, you need to act in minutes."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A promo bug gave away money. The fix needed a new app version. Abuse continued for two days while the update rolled out."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Add a remote settings table (or Remote Config) that the app reads on start. Include: an on/off switch for every risky feature (payments, promos, sign-up, uploads, AI features), a minimum supported app version, and a message to show users. If the app version is below the minimum, show an update screen with a button to Google Play (use Google Play In-App Updates). Only admins can change these settings. Show me it working."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I switched a feature off from the admin side and the app hid it within minutes (I tested).","Setting the minimum version above my current version shows the update screen (I tested).","Only admins can change these settings (A-vs-B style test).","RUNBOOK.md says when and how to use each switch."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"No set of limits stops every abuser. Determined fraudsters adapt. The goal is to make abuse expensive and slow, to notice it fast (Chapter 16 alerts), and to switch things off quickly. Expect to tune limits after launch."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Get ABUSE_LIMITS.md written and check every number makes sense for real users.","Set spending limits at your SMS provider and on every paid service today.","Build and test the kill switch and force-update before launch, not after.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 10."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — ABUSE PROTECTION (Chapter 9)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ Bots and abusers find the app expensive and slow to abuse, and you\nI decide         can switch things off in minutes.\nThe AI does\nProof I demand   ☐ Limit numbers for each action\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Offer rules\nRed flags        ☐ What happens on a bad Play Integrity verdict\nDone when"},{"t":"check","items":["Writes ABUSE_LIMITS.md","Builds server-side limits and tests","Integrates Play Integrity","Builds kill switch and force-update","Limit tests refusing extra requests","Kill switch test on my phone","Force-update screen test","SMS spending limit screenshot","What can a bot repeat here?","What does this cost me per 1,000 abuses?","How fast can I switch this off?","Limits only inside the app","OTP open to all countries","Rewards before verification","No way to switch off a feature","Every action has a tested limit","OTP protected","Kill switch and force-update tested"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"10","title":"Making the app fast and smooth","page":71,"sections":[{"id":"10","title":"Introduction","page":71,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Most of your users have budget phones and patchy internet. Build and test for them, not for the AI’s ideal phone."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: “I made it faster” without numbers means nothing. Speed work without measuring first usually changes the wrong thing. Rule for this chapter: measure, fix one thing, measure again."}]},{"id":"10.1","title":"The six things to measure","page":71,"blocks":[{"t":"pre","text":"What                    Plain meaning                           How you notice it\nStartup time                                                    Users wait on a splash screen\n                        Time from tapping the icon to a usable\nSlow frames (jank)      screen                                  Lists jump and feel heavy"},{"t":"h","text":"Scrolling or animations that stutter"},{"t":"pre","text":"Memory                  How much phone memory the app uses App restarts or crashes on cheap\nApp size                                                                         phones\n                        Download and install size               Users skip big downloads on\n                                                                mobile data\nBattery                 Power used, especially in the           Users see your app in battery\nData use                background                              settings and uninstall\n                        Mobile data used                        Users on small data packs avoid\n                                                                the app"}]},{"id":"10.2","title":"Google’s own quality bar","page":71,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Google Play watches your app’s real-world quality in Android vitals (inside Play Console). If you cross certain “bad behaviour” limits, Play can show your app to fewer people, and may show a warning on your store page."},{"t":"pre","text":"Core vital              Bad behaviour line             Plain meaning\n                        (overall)\nUser-perceived crash rate 1.09% of daily users         About 1 in 100 users seeing a crash per\n                                                       day is already too many\nUser-perceived ANR rate 0.47% of daily users           ANR = “App Not Responding”: the app\n                                                       freezes and Android offers to close it\nExcessive partial wake  5% of sessions                 The app keeps the phone awake in the\nlocks                                                  background too long, draining battery"},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"These thresholds are from Google’s Android vitals page. Google adds new vitals from time to time (the wake-lock vital is recent). Where to check: developer.android.com/topic/performance/vitals and Play Console → Android vitals. Read on 22 Nov 2026."}]},{"id":"10.3","title":"Measure first: your speed baseline","page":72,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Recording today’s numbers for the six measures, on a cheap phone and a slow network, before changing anything. Like: weighing yourself before starting a diet. Without the first number, you cannot know if anything worked."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Without a baseline, the AI “optimises” randomly, sometimes making things worse, and you pay for it."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI rewrote the image loading “for speed”. Nobody measured. Startup was actually slower, and a new bug appeared."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Before changing anything, measure and record in SPEED_REPORT.md: cold startup time, slow frames while scrolling the main list, memory use, app download size, battery and data use for a 10-minute session. Use Android’s official tools (Macrobenchmark, Android Studio profiler, Play Console Android vitals). Test on a low-end phone profile with a slow 3G-like network. Explain each number in plain words."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["SPEED_REPORT.md has a dated “before” number for each measure.","The test phone was low-end (2–4 GB RAM), not a flagship.","A slow network was tested.","Each number has a plain-words explanation."]}]},{"id":"10.4","title":"Target numbers for cheap phones and slow networks","page":72,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"These are sensible targets for budget phones. Google’s own guidance aims for very fast cold starts on good devices; Android vitals flags a cold start of 5 seconds or more as excessive. Write your own targets on the Speed sheet in the print pack."},{"t":"pre","text":"Measure                        Good target (budget phone)         Danger sign\nCold startup to usable screen\nScrolling main list            Under 2 seconds                    5 seconds or more\nApp download size\n                               Smooth, very few slow frames       Visible stutter\nFirst screen on slow network\n                               As small as possible; under 30 MB  Over 100 MB for a simple\nBackground battery             is a good aim                      app\n                               Shows something useful (cached     Blank screen or endless\n                               data or skeleton) within 2–3 s     spinner\n                               Nothing running when not needed    App appears high in\n                                                                  battery settings"},{"t":"label","text":"THESE ARE GUIDELINES, NOT LAWS"},{"t":"p","text":"The targets above are practical aims for budget phones, not official Google numbers (except the 5-second line). Your app’s best target depends on what it does. The rule that matters: every change must make the measured numbers better."}]},{"id":"10.5","title":"The usual causes, and the usual fixes","page":73,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Most slow Android apps are slow for the same few reasons. The AI should check these first."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a car mechanic checks tyres, fuel and battery before taking the engine apart."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Fixing common causes first gives the biggest gains for the least risk."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Hours spent on clever tricks while the real cause was a 5 MB photo loaded for each small thumbnail."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Check these common causes, in this order, and report which apply with evidence: (1) heavy work during startup, (2) work on the main thread (network, database, big files), (3) large images not resized or cached, (4) lists that are not lazy or redraw too much, (5) too many network calls or no caching, (6) unused libraries and resources making the app big, (7) background work that runs too often. Fix ONE cause at a time and measure after each."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The AI gave evidence (a measurement or tool screenshot) for each cause it found.","Only one fix per step, each with its own save point.","After each fix, SPEED_REPORT.md has a new number."]},{"t":"h","text":"Standard fixes the AI should know"},{"t":"p","text":"• Baseline Profiles: a Google tool that makes startup and scrolling faster by preparing the most-used code in advance."},{"t":"p","text":"• R8 shrinking: removes unused code and makes the app smaller (also helps security, Chapter 8)."},{"t":"p","text":"• Lazy lists in Compose (LazyColumn) with stable keys, so only visible items are drawn."},{"t":"p","text":"• An image library with caching and resizing (for example Coil), and images served at the right size from the server."},{"t":"p","text":"• Local caching so the app shows saved data instantly and refreshes in the background."},{"t":"p","text":"• WorkManager for background jobs, so Android can run them at good times for the battery."}]},{"id":"10.6","title":"Before-and-after proof","page":74,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A simple table: each measure, the number before, the number after, and what changed. Like: the doctor’s report comparing this month’s blood test to last month’s."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"It proves the work was worth paying for, and catches “improvements” that made things worse."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The AI said “40% faster”. When measured again by a fresh AI, nothing had changed."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Update SPEED_REPORT.md with a before-and-after table for every measure, same phone profile, same network, same steps. Run each test at least 5 times and give the middle (median) value. Flag any measure that got worse."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Before and after numbers use the same phone and network.","Each number is a median of 5 or more runs.","Nothing got worse, or I accepted the trade-off in writing.","I felt the difference myself on a cheap phone."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"Lab tests are not real life. Real users have older phones, full storage and busy networks. After launch, Android vitals and crash tools (Chapter 16) show the truth. Keep a cheap Android phone for testing; it is the best speed tool you can own. ➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Get a “before” SPEED_REPORT.md on a low-end phone profile and slow network.","Write your targets on the Speed sheet in the print pack.","Fix one cause at a time, with before-and-after numbers.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 11."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — SPEED (Chapter 10)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ Fast start, smooth scrolling and small size on budget phones and\nI decide         slow networks, proven with numbers.\nThe AI does"},{"t":"check","items":["Target numbers"]},{"t":"pre","text":"Proof I demand   ☐ Which trade-offs are acceptable\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Which test phone to use\nRed flags"},{"t":"check","items":["Measures the baseline"]},{"t":"pre","text":"Done when        ☐ Finds causes with evidence"},{"t":"check","items":["Fixes one cause at a time","Updates SPEED_REPORT.md","Dated before-and-after table","Median of 5+ runs","Android vitals below bad-behaviour lines","What is the number before and after?","Was this measured on a cheap phone and slow network?","Did anything get worse?","“Faster” with no number","Many changes at once","Tests only on a flagship or emulator default","Blank screens on slow networks","Targets met or accepted","Report saved","Vitals healthy in closed test"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"11","title":"Edge cases: the complete list","page":77,"sections":[{"id":"11","title":"Introduction","page":77,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An edge case is anything that is not the normal, happy path. Real life is mostly edge cases."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: the AI builds and tests the happy path: good internet, one tap, a new phone, a patient user. Your real users are on a train with weak signal, tapping twice, with a phone full of photos. Every row in this chapter is a real bug that has hit real apps."}]},{"id":"11.1","title":"How to use this chapter","page":77,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A full list of tricky situations, grouped by type, each with a simple test you can do yourself on your phone."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a pilot’s checklist: boring, complete, and the reason planes rarely crash."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Edge-case bugs cause most 1-star reviews: “I was charged twice”, “my order disappeared”, “it crashes when I open it”."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A user tapped “Pay” twice on a slow network. Two orders, two charges, one angry review and a refund fight."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Go through every edge case in my handbook’s list (pasted below) for my app. For each one, tell me if it applies, what our app does today, and whether there is an automated test. Build the missing handling and tests one group at a time. Then give me a short manual test script I can follow on my phone for each group."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every row below is marked: does not apply / handled + tested / to do.","Every “handled” row has an automated test name.","I did the manual phone test for every group myself."]}]},{"id":"11.1-1","title":"Group A: Internet and network","page":77,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Edge case","What can go wrong","How YOU test it"],"tick":true,"rows":[["No internet at all","Blank screen, crash, lost input","Turn on Airplane mode, then open every screen"],["Very slow internet","Endless spinner, timeouts, double requests","Use a slow network (or a weak-signal spot) and do the main task"],["Internet drops mid-action","Half-saved order, payment unclear","Start an action, switch on Airplane mode halfway"],["Wi-Fi with no real internet","App thinks it is online","Connect to a hotspot that has no data"],["Switching Wi-Fi ↔ mobile data","Request fails silently","Walk out of Wi-Fi range during an action"],["Server down or very slow","Crash or confusing error","Ask the AI to simulate the server failing in a test build"]],"text":"No internet at all Blank screen, crash, lost input Turn on Airplane mode, then open every screen Very slow internet Endless spinner, timeouts, double requests Use a slow network (or a weak-signal spot) and do the main task Internet drops mid-action Half-saved order, payment unclear Start an action, switch on Airplane mode halfway Wi-Fi with no real internet App thinks it is online Connect to a hotspot that has no data Switching Wi-Fi ↔ mobile data Request fails silently Walk out of Wi-Fi range during an action Server down or very slow Crash or confusing error Ask the AI to simulate the server failing in a test build"},{"t":"p","text":"main task"},{"t":"p","text":"server failing in a test build"}]},{"id":"11.1-2","title":"Group B: Taps and timing","page":78,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Edge case","What can go wrong","How YOU test it"],"tick":true,"rows":[["Double-tap or rapid taps","Duplicate orders, payments, posts","Tap every important button 5 times fast"],["Back button during an action","Action half-done, wrong screen","Press back while something is loading"],["Leaving and returning mid-flow","Form data lost","Fill a form, open another app, come back"],["Two actions at once","Wrong totals, mixed data","Change cart quantity while it is still saving"]],"text":"Double-tap or rapid taps Duplicate orders, payments, posts Tap every important button 5 times fast Back button during an action Action half-done, wrong screen Press back while something is loading Leaving and returning mid-flow Form data lost Fill a form, open another app, come back Two actions at once Wrong totals, mixed data Change cart quantity while it is still saving"},{"t":"p","text":"Change cart quantity while it ☐ is still saving"}]},{"id":"11.1-3","title":"Group C: The app being killed or paused","page":78,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Edge case","What can go wrong","How YOU test it"],"tick":true,"rows":[["Android kills the app in background","User returns to a crash or empty screen","Turn on “Don’t keep activities” in Developer options, switch apps, return"],["Phone call or notification mid-task","Lost progress","Call your phone during checkout"],["Screen rotation / split screen / foldable","Data lost, layout broken","Rotate the phone on every screen"],["Battery saver / low battery","Background work never runs","Turn on battery saver and test sync and notifications"],["Phone restarts","Scheduled work or reminders lost","Restart the phone, check reminders and pending uploads"]],"text":"Android kills the app in background User returns to a crash or empty screen Turn on “Don’t keep activities” in Developer options, switch apps, return Phone call or notification mid-task Lost progress Call your phone during checkout Screen rotation / split screen / foldable Data lost, layout broken Rotate the phone on every screen Battery saver / low battery Background work never runs Turn on battery saver and test sync and notifications Phone restarts Scheduled work or reminders lost Restart the phone, check reminders and pending uploads"},{"t":"p","text":"options, switch apps, return"},{"t":"p","text":"test sync and notifications"},{"t":"p","text":"uploads"}]},{"id":"11.1-4","title":"Group D: Permissions","page":79,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Edge case","What can go wrong","How YOU test it"],"tick":true,"rows":[["User denies a permission","Crash or dead button","Deny camera / location / notifications and use the feature"],["User denies “forever”","App asks again and again, or nothing happens","Deny twice, then try the feature"],["Permission removed later in Settings","Crash on next use","Remove it in phone Settings, return to the app"],["Only approximate location given","Wrong results","Choose “approximate” when asked"],["Notifications turned off","User misses important updates silently","Turn off notifications, check the app explains it"]],"text":"User denies a permission Crash or dead button Deny camera / location / notifications and use the feature User denies “forever” App asks again and again, or nothing happens Deny twice, then try the feature Permission removed later in Settings Crash on next use Remove it in phone Settings, return to the app Only approximate location given Wrong results Choose “approximate” when asked Notifications turned off User misses important updates silently Turn off notifications, check the app explains it"},{"t":"p","text":"notifications and use the"},{"t":"p","text":"feature"}]},{"id":"11.1-5","title":"Group E: Time, dates and money formats","page":79,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Edge case","What can go wrong","How YOU test it"],"tick":true,"rows":[["Phone clock set wrong","Offers expire early, OTP fails, wrong order times","Set the phone date one day ahead and back"],["Different time zone","Times shown wrong","Change phone time zone"],["Midnight, month-end, leap day","Daily limits or reports break","Ask the AI for tests around 23:59, 31st and 29 Feb"],["12-hour vs 24-hour clock","Times confusing","Switch the phone setting"],["Rupee and number formats","Wrong commas or decimals in prices","Check prices like ₹1,00,000 and ₹99.50"]],"text":"Phone clock set wrong Offers expire early, OTP fails, wrong order times Set the phone date one day ahead and back Different time zone Times shown wrong Change phone time zone Midnight, month-end, leap day Daily limits or reports break Ask the AI for tests around 23:59, 31st and 29 Feb 12-hour vs 24-hour clock Times confusing Switch the phone setting Rupee and number formats Wrong commas or decimals in prices Check prices like ₹1,00,000 and ₹99.50"},{"t":"label","text":"RULE FOR TIME"},{"t":"p","text":"Server time decides everything important (expiry, limits, order times). Never trust the phone’s clock for money or rules."}]},{"id":"11.1-6","title":"Group F: Accounts and devices","page":80,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Edge case","What can go wrong","How YOU test it"],"tick":true,"rows":[["Same account on two phones","Data overwritten, cart mixed","Log in on two phones, change the same thing"],["Logged out while using the app","Crash or private data still shown","Log out everywhere from phone 1, use phone 2"],["Session expires","Lost work, confusing error","Leave the app overnight, then continue"],["Account deleted","Old data still shown, crashes","Delete a test account, reopen the app"],["Account banned or blocked by admin","User can still act","Block a test user in admin, try to use the app"],["Two people share one phone","Second person sees first person’s data","Log out, log in as another user, check everything"]],"text":"Same account on two phones Data overwritten, cart mixed Log in on two phones, change the same thing Logged out while using the app Crash or private data still shown Log out everywhere from phone 1, use phone 2 Session expires Lost work, confusing error Leave the app overnight, then continue Account deleted Old data still shown, crashes Delete a test account, reopen the app Account banned or blocked by admin User can still act Block a test user in admin, try to use the app Two people share one phone Second person sees first person’s data Log out, log in as another user, check everything"},{"t":"p","text":"Delete a test account, reopen ☐ the app"}]},{"id":"11.1-7","title":"Group G: App versions and the server","page":80,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Edge case","What can go wrong","How YOU test it"],"tick":true,"rows":[["Old app version still in use","Crashes after database or server change","Keep an old build installed and use it after each change"],["Update installed over old version","Saved data lost or broken","Install old version, add data, update, check"],["Feature switched off (kill switch)","Crash or broken screen","Turn the switch off in admin, use the app"],["Force-update needed","User stuck with no way forward","Raise minimum version in admin, open the app"]],"text":"Old app version still in use Crashes after database or server change Keep an old build installed and use it after each change Update installed over old version Saved data lost or broken Install old version, add data, update, check Feature switched off (kill switch) Crash or broken screen Turn the switch off in admin, use the app Force-update needed User stuck with no way forward Raise minimum version in admin, open the app"},{"t":"p","text":"admin, open the app"}]},{"id":"11.1-8","title":"Group H: Data and typing","page":80,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Edge case","What can go wrong","How YOU test it"],"tick":true,"rows":[["Empty fields","Crash or saved blank records","Submit every form empty"],["Very long text","Layout breaks, server error","Paste a very long paragraph into every field"],["Emojis, Hindi and other scripts","Garbled text, search fails","Type emojis and Hindi everywhere"],["Spaces and special characters","Login or search fails The App Builder's Handbook ·","Add spaces before/after; try symbols like ’ “ & < > page 80"],["Huge or wrong files","Crash, huge bills, harmful files","Upload a very large photo and a non-image file"],["Zero, negative or huge numbers","Free items, negative totals","Try quantity 0, −1, 99999"],["Empty lists and first-time use","Blank screens","Use a brand-new account on every screen"],["Very large lists","Slow or crash","Ask the AI to load 10,000 test items in development"]],"text":"Empty fields Crash or saved blank records Submit every form empty Very long text Layout breaks, server error Paste a very long paragraph into every field Emojis, Hindi and other scripts Garbled text, search fails Type emojis and Hindi everywhere Spaces and special characters Login or search fails The App Builder's Handbook · Add spaces before/after; try symbols like ’ “ & < > page 80 Huge or wrong files Crash, huge bills, harmful files Upload a very large photo and a non-image file Zero, negative or huge numbers Free items, negative totals Try quantity 0, −1, 99999 Empty lists and first-time use Blank screens Use a brand-new account on every screen Very large lists Slow or crash Ask the AI to load 10,000 test items in development"},{"t":"p","text":"Paste a very long paragraph ☐ into every field"},{"t":"p","text":"and a non-image file"}]},{"id":"11.1-9","title":"Group I: The phone itself","page":81,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Edge case","What can go wrong","How YOU test it"],"tick":true,"rows":[["Phone storage almost full","Crash when saving or downloading","Fill storage, use the app"],["Low memory phone (2–3 GB)","Restarts, slow","Test on a cheap phone"],["Largest font and display size","Text cut off, buttons hidden","Set font and display size to maximum"],["Dark mode","Invisible text","Switch dark mode on"],["Small and large screens, tablets","Layout broken","Test on a small phone and a tablet (or emulator)"],["Older Android versions you support","Crashes on features not available","Test on the oldest Android version you allow"]],"text":"Phone storage almost full Crash when saving or downloading Fill storage, use the app Low memory phone (2–3 GB) Restarts, slow Test on a cheap phone Largest font and display size Text cut off, buttons hidden Set font and display size to maximum Dark mode Invisible text Switch dark mode on Small and large screens, tablets Layout broken Test on a small phone and a tablet (or emulator) Older Android versions you support Crashes on features not available Test on the oldest Android version you allow"}]},{"id":"11.1-10","title":"Group J: Money (if your app takes payments)","page":81,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Edge case","What can go wrong","How YOU test it"],"tick":true,"rows":[["Payment succeeds but app closes","Order not marked paid","Close the app right after paying"],["Payment fails or is cancelled","Order stuck or marked paid","Cancel on the payment screen"],["Payment confirmation arrives late","Duplicate order or wrong status","Ask the AI to simulate a delayed webhook"],["Refund and partial refund","Wrong amounts","Do a test refund in the gateway’s test mode"],["Price changed while in cart","User pays old price","Change price in admin while item is in a test cart"]],"text":"Payment succeeds but app closes Order not marked paid Close the app right after paying Payment fails or is cancelled Order stuck or marked paid Cancel on the payment screen Payment confirmation arrives late Duplicate order or wrong status Ask the AI to simulate a delayed webhook Refund and partial refund Wrong amounts Do a test refund in the gateway’s test mode Price changed while in cart User pays old price Change price in admin while item is in a test cart"},{"t":"p","text":"gateway’s test mode"}]},{"id":"11.1-11","title":"Group K: People","page":77,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Edge case","What can go wrong","How YOU test it"],"tick":true,"rows":[["Users under 18","Legal and Play policy problems","Decide your age policy (Chapter 13) and test the age gate"],["Screen reader (TalkBack) users","Cannot use the app","Turn on TalkBack and do the main task"],["Users with no email","Cannot sign up","Check other sign-in options work"],["Users who make mistakes","No way to undo","Delete something by mistake; is there undo or confirm?"]],"text":"Users under 18 Legal and Play policy problems Decide your age policy (Chapter 13) and test the age gate Screen reader (TalkBack) users Cannot use the app Turn on TalkBack and do the main task Users with no email Cannot sign up Check other sign-in options work Users who make mistakes No way to undo Delete something by mistake; is there undo or confirm?"},{"t":"p","text":"(Chapter 13) and test the age"},{"t":"p","text":"gate"},{"t":"prompt","text":"Here is my edge-case list. For my app, make an EDGE_CASES.md with one row per case: applies (yes/no), what the app does now, automated test name, status. Then build missing handling one group at a time, starting with Group B (taps and timing) and Group J (money), because they cost the most when they fail.","title":"EDGE-CASE SWEEP"},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"No list is complete for every app. Your app will have its own special edge cases. Add them to EDGE_CASES.md as you find them, especially from user complaints after launch."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Print the Edge-case tick sheet from the print pack.","Run the edge-case sweep prompt, then do the manual phone tests group by group.","Fix money and double-tap problems first.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 12."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — EDGE CASES (Chapter 11)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ The app behaves sensibly when things are not normal.\nI decide\nThe AI does      ☐ Which groups apply\nProof I demand   ☐ Order of fixing (money first)\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Oldest Android version supported\nRed flags\nDone when        ☐ Writes EDGE_CASES.md"},{"t":"check","items":["Builds handling and automated tests","Gives manual test scripts","EDGE_CASES.md with test names","My own phone tests ticked","No duplicate orders on 5 fast taps","What happens if the internet drops right now?","What if the user taps twice?","What if the app is killed here?","Only happy-path tests","Phone clock trusted for rules","Blank screens on first use","All groups checked","All applicable rows handled and tested","Edge-case sheet ticked"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"12","title":"Testing: try to break it before users do","page":84,"sections":[{"id":"12","title":"Introduction","page":84,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Tests are automatic checks that run every time the code changes. They are your guard dogs."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: an AI that wrote the code and also wrote the tests is marking its own homework. It will test what it built, not what it forgot. That is why this chapter ends with a second, independent AI whose only job is to find faults, and with tests YOU do on a real phone."},{"t":"h","text":"The test pyramid in one picture"},{"t":"pre","text":"Level              What it checks                       Kitchen picture                    How many\nUnit tests         One small piece of logic (e.g.       Tasting one ingredient             Many\n                   “discount is calculated correctly”)\nIntegration tests  Pieces working together (app +       Tasting the finished               Some\n                   database + rules)                    sauce\nUI tests           Screens and taps, like a robot user A test customer                     A few, for main\n                                                                     ordering a full meal  flows\nSecurity tests     Attacks fail (A-vs-B, change-the-    Trying to sneak into the One per lock\n                   ID, limits)                          kitchen\nLoad tests         Many users at once                   Lunch rush with 100                Before launch,\n                                                        customers                          then before big\n                                                                                           events\nManual tests on    How it really feels                  The owner eating the               Every release\nreal phones                                             meal"}]},{"id":"12.1","title":"Unit tests","page":84,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Small automatic tests for single pieces of logic: price totals, discounts, date rules, input checks."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: checking that each scale in the shop weighs correctly."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"They are fast and catch mistakes in money and rules instantly, every time anything changes."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A change to rounding made every ₹99.50 item cost ₹99. Nobody noticed for a month."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Write unit tests for every rule involving money, dates, limits and permissions. Include normal values, edge values (zero, negative, very large, empty) and wrong values. Show me the list of test names in plain English."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every money and rule calculation has tests.","Test names read like sentences I understand (e.g. “discount never makes the total negative”).","They run automatically in CI (Chapter 7)."]}]},{"id":"12.2","title":"Integration tests","page":85,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Tests that run the app’s logic against a real test database with the real access rules switched on. Like: testing the whole kitchen line together, not each cook alone."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Most serious bugs live in the joins: the app expects one thing, the database rule does another."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Each part worked alone. Together, a new database rule silently blocked order saving, and the app showed “success” anyway."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Write integration tests that run against a local or development Supabase with all RLS rules on. Cover: sign-up, login, the main task end to end, and the User A vs User B tests from Chapter 6. Never run tests against production."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Integration tests use a development or local database, never production.","RLS is ON during these tests.","The main task is tested end to end."]}]},{"id":"12.3","title":"UI tests (a robot taps through the app)","page":86,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Automatic tests that open the app, tap buttons, type text and check what appears on screen. Like: a mystery shopper who follows a script every single day."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"They catch broken screens and buttons that unit tests cannot see."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A layout change hid the “Place order” button behind the keyboard on small phones. Orders dropped by half."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Write Compose UI tests for the main flows (sign-up, main task, payment if any, account deletion), on a small screen and a large font size. Include the error and offline states. Run them in CI."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Main flows each have a UI test.","At least one run uses a small screen and large font.","Error and offline states are covered."]}]},{"id":"12.4","title":"Security tests","page":86,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Tests that try to attack your own app and must fail: other users’ data, changed IDs, admin actions as a normal user, limits, fake payment confirmations. Like: hiring someone to try to break into your shop, then fixing what they find."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Security bugs are invisible in normal use. Only attack-style tests find them before attackers do."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"All normal tests passed. Nobody tested “user B opens user A’s invoice”. It worked."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Collect all security tests from Chapters 6, 8 and 9 into one security test suite that runs in CI: A-vs-B for every private table and bucket, change-the-ID, normal-user-tries-admin, rate limits, fake webhook, expired session. Show me the full list and the result."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["One security suite exists and runs in CI.","It covers every private table and storage bucket.","All attacks fail (that is a pass)."]}]},{"id":"12.5","title":"Load tests (many users at once)","page":87,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Simulating many users using the app at the same time, to see when it slows down or breaks. Like: a fire drill: you find the blocked exit before the real fire."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"A sale, an influencer post or a festival can bring 50 times the normal traffic in an hour. You want to know your limit before that day."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A Diwali offer went viral. The database hit its limit, every screen timed out, and the sale day was lost."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Plan a load test on a staging copy (never production) for the main actions. Before running it, check our backend provider’s rules on load testing and tell me the cost. Report: how many users at once we handle comfortably, where it breaks, and the cheapest fix."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The test ran on staging, not production.","The provider’s load-testing rules were checked first.","I know our comfortable limit in users at once.","A plan exists for a big traffic day (upgrade, kill switch for heavy features)."]}]},{"id":"12.6","title":"Red-team: the AI attacks its own app","page":87,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"You ask the AI to switch roles and act as an attacker, a fraudster and an angry user, and to try every trick against the app. Like: a chess player who plays the other side of the board to find their own weak moves."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"It finds abuse paths that normal testing misses, especially money and offer abuse."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Nobody thought about cancelling an order after the cashback was paid. Users did it on purpose."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Act as three attackers: (1) a hacker who wants user data, (2) a fraudster who wants free money or goods, (3) a bot owner who wants to spam or drain my bills. For each, list 10 concrete attacks against THIS app, try each one in the development environment, and report which ones worked. Do not fix yet."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I received 30 attack ideas, with which ones worked.","Every attack that worked became a fix plus a test.","Money and offer abuse were included."]}]},{"id":"12.7","title":"The independent second-AI audit","page":88,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A brand-new AI chat (or a different AI tool) that has never seen your build chats is given your project and asked to find everything wrong. It does not trust anything the first AI said. Like: a second doctor’s opinion, from a doctor who has not read the first doctor’s notes."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"The building AI has blind spots and a reason to believe its work is good. A fresh auditor does not."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The building AI said every check passed. A fresh audit found the admin panel used the secret key in the browser."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Use the HOSTILE AUDITOR prompt from Chapter 19 in a brand-new chat. Give it the project files, CLAUDE.md, the handbook checklists, and nothing from the building chats."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The audit ran in a fresh chat that never saw the build chats.","Every finding is listed with severity and proof.","High findings were fixed and then RE-checked by the auditor.","The final audit report is saved in the project as AUDIT_REPORT_[date].md."]}]},{"id":"12.8","title":"Your own real-phone test (every release)","page":89,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Nothing replaces you holding a real, cheap phone. Before every release, do this 20- minute script:"},{"t":"check","items":["Install the release build from the Play testing track (not from the AI’s computer).","Sign up as a brand-new user. Check first-time and empty screens.","Do the main task three times: on Wi-Fi, on mobile data, and with Airplane mode switched on halfway.","Tap the main button 5 times fast. Check for duplicates.","Switch dark mode and the largest font, and look at every screen.","Log out, log in as a second user, and check you see nothing of the first.","Delete the account. Check you cannot log in and the data is gone.","Note anything slow, confusing or ugly in PROJECT_LOG.md."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"Tests prove the presence of the checks you thought of, not the absence of all bugs. Coverage numbers (“90% of code tested”) can look impressive while testing nothing important. Judge tests by WHAT they check, using the lists in this chapter."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Ask for the full test list in plain English, grouped by level.","Run the red-team prompt, then the hostile auditor in a fresh chat.","Do the 20-minute real-phone test yourself before every release.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 13."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — TESTING (Chapter 12)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ Every important rule, flow and lock is checked automatically, attacked\nI decide         on purpose, and audited by a fresh AI.\nThe AI does\nProof I demand   ☐ Which flows are “main”"},{"t":"check","items":["When findings are severe enough to delay launch"]},{"t":"p","text":"Questions I ask  ☐ Load test budget Red flags"},{"t":"check","items":["Writes unit, integration, UI and security tests"]},{"t":"pre","text":"Done when        ☐ Runs red-team attacks"},{"t":"check","items":["Keeps CI green","Plain-English test list","CI green with 0 skipped","Red-team report","Fresh-AI audit report","What does this test actually check?","Which attacks worked?","What did the fresh auditor find?","Tests changed to pass","Skipped tests","Tests against production","Only the building AI reviewed the work","All levels present","Audit highs fixed and re-checked","My real-phone test passed"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"13","title":"Privacy, legal and Google Play policy","page":91,"sections":[{"id":"13","title":"Introduction","page":91,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Google can remove your app. Indian law can fine you. Users can stop trusting you. This chapter keeps all three on your side."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: this book is not legal advice, and neither is any AI. AI- written privacy policies often promise things the app does not do, which is worse than having a short honest one. Use this chapter to get everything ready, then have a lawyer review it if you handle money, health data, children’s data, or lots of users."}]},{"id":"13.1","title":"Collect the minimum","page":91,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Only collect personal data you truly need for a feature the user asked for (Chapter 6 started this with DATA_MAP.md). Like: a shop that asks for your phone number only if you want home delivery."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Less data means less legal work, smaller forms, lower risk in a breach, and more user trust. India’s DPDP law is built on purpose limits and collecting only what is needed."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An app asked for contacts and location “for a better experience”. Google Play flagged it, users gave 1-star reviews, and none of that data was used."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Review DATA_MAP.md and every Android permission the app asks for. For each, tell me the exact feature that needs it. Remove anything without a clear feature. Ask for permissions only at the moment the feature is used, with a one-line explanation first."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every permission maps to a feature I can name.","No permission is asked on first launch unless the app cannot work without it.","At least one unneeded item was removed."]}]},{"id":"13.2","title":"Privacy policy and terms","page":91,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A privacy policy is a public web page that explains what data you collect, why, who you share it with, how long you keep it, and how users can use their rights. Terms of service are the rules for using your app."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: the notice board at a clinic explaining how patient files are handled, and the rules of the clinic."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Google Play requires a privacy policy for every app, even apps that collect nothing. Users and the law expect one that is accurate."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An AI-written policy said “we never share data with third parties”. The app used a crash- reporting and analytics service. That is sharing."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Write a privacy policy in simple English based ONLY on DATA_MAP.md, DEPENDENCIES.md and our real outside services. Include: who we are and how to contact us (grievance contact), what we collect and why, every outside service that receives data, how long we keep it, user rights (access, correction, deletion, withdraw consent), children, security, breach notice, and changes. Mark anything you are unsure about. Also write short terms of service. Both must be hosted on a public web page."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every outside service in DEPENDENCIES.md is named in the policy.","Nothing in the policy is untrue for the current app.","It has a real contact for privacy complaints.","The link works without logging in, and is set in Play Console and inside the app."]}]},{"id":"13.3","title":"Notice and consent (India DPDP)","page":92,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Before collecting personal data, users must get a clear, separate notice of what you collect and why, and give consent that they can withdraw as easily as they gave it. Like: a doctor explaining a procedure in plain words and getting your signature, and letting you change your mind."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"This is the core of India’s DPDP Act. Penalties for serious failures are very large. Clear consent also builds user trust."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"One pre-ticked box said “I agree to everything”. Withdrawing consent required emailing support and waiting weeks."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Design a consent screen for first use: a short plain notice listing each kind of personal data and its purpose, a link to the full privacy policy, and no pre-ticked boxes. Optional uses (like marketing) need their own separate choice. Add a “Privacy” screen in settings where users can see what they agreed to, withdraw optional consent, download or correct their data, and delete their account. Record when and to what each user agreed."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["No pre-ticked boxes.","Optional uses have separate switches.","Withdrawing consent is as easy as giving it (I tested).","Consent records are stored with date and version."]},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"India’s DPDP Rules were notified in November 2025. The main duties for app makers (notice, consent, security safeguards, breach reporting, children’s data, user rights) are scheduled to apply from about 13–14 May 2027. The Rules require a detailed breach report to the Data Protection Board within 72 hours, and verifiable parental consent before processing a child’s data (under 18). Where to check: meity.gov.in (DPDP Act and Rules) and pib.gov.in. Read on 22 Nov"},{"t":"p","text":"2026. Ask a lawyer for anything uncertain."}]},{"id":"13.4","title":"Account and data deletion (Google Play rule)","page":93,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"If users can create an account in your app, they must be able to ask for the account and its data to be deleted, both inside the app and from a web page. Like: closing a bank account: you can do it at the branch or online, and the bank must actually close it."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Google Play requires it, and asks for the web link in the Data safety form. DPDP also gives users the right to erasure."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An app had “Delete account” only inside the app. A user who had already uninstalled it had no way to delete their data, and complained to Google."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Build account deletion in two places: (1) inside the app in settings, (2) a public web page where a user can request deletion without installing the app (with identity check). Both must delete the account and its data as built in Chapter 6. Tell the user what will be deleted, what is kept by law and for how long, and confirm when done."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["In-app deletion works (I tested with a test account).","The web deletion page works without the app.","Users are told what is deleted and what is kept.","The web link is entered in Play Console’s Data safety form."]}]},{"id":"13.5","title":"The Data safety form in Play Console","page":94,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A form in Play Console where you declare what data the app collects, shares, why, and how it is protected. Google shows a summary on your store page."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: the ingredients and allergy label on a food packet."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"It is required for every app, including testing tracks. Wrong answers can lead to rejection or removal. It must include data collected by libraries and outside services inside your app."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The form said “no data collected”. A crash-reporting library collected device information. Google sent a policy warning."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Prepare my Data safety answers as a table I can copy into Play Console: for each data type Google lists, say collected (yes/no), shared (yes/no), required or optional, purpose, and which library or service causes it. Use DATA_MAP.md, DEPENDENCIES.md and each library’s own published Data safety guidance. Mark anything unsure."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I filled the form myself in Play Console from the AI’s table.","Every library’s data is included.","Answers match the privacy policy exactly.","I update the form whenever a new library or data type is added."]},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"Google Play policies (User Data, Data safety, account deletion, permissions) are updated several times a year. Where to check: Play Console → Policy → App content, and the Google Play Policy Center. Read on 22 Nov 2026."}]},{"id":"13.6","title":"Children and under-18 users","page":95,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Rules that apply if children may use your app. In India’s DPDP law, a “child” is anyone under 18. Google Play has a separate Families policy for apps aimed at children. Like: a cinema checking ages for an adult film, and asking parents for permission for school trips."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Children’s data has the strictest rules and the biggest penalties. Getting this wrong can end the app."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A study app for teenagers collected names and photos without parental consent and used normal ad libraries not approved for children."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Help me decide our age policy. If the app is not for under-18s, add an age check at sign-up and explain how we handle under-18 users who get through. If it IS for under-18s, list everything needed: verifiable parental consent, no tracking or targeted ads, Play Families policy, approved libraries only. Tell me plainly if I need a lawyer before launch."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I set the target age in Play Console honestly.","There is an age policy written in PROJECT_BRIEF.md.","If children may use it: a lawyer has reviewed the plan BEFORE launch."]}]},{"id":"13.7","title":"If data leaks: the breach plan","page":95,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A written plan for what to do if personal data is exposed: stop it, find out what happened, tell the authorities and the users. Like: a fire plan on the wall: who calls whom, which exits, where to meet."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Under DPDP, you must inform affected users and the Data Protection Board, with a detailed report within 72 hours. Panic wastes those hours."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A leak was found on Friday night. Nobody knew who to tell or what to say. By Monday it was in the news, and the legal deadline had passed."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Write a breach section in RUNBOOK.md: how to stop the leak (rotate keys, switch off features), how to find what was exposed (logs), who to inform and by when (Data Protection Board, affected users, Google if needed), a plain-language message template for users, and who to call for legal help."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["RUNBOOK.md has a breach section I understand.","A user message template is ready.","I know where logs are and how long they are kept.","I have the contact of a lawyer or advisor."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"Laws and policies change, and they apply differently to each business. This chapter prepares you; it does not make you compliant on its own. A one-time review by a lawyer who knows Indian data law costs far less than one penalty."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Remove any data or permission you cannot justify.","Publish the privacy policy, terms and web deletion page.","Fill in the Data safety form from the AI’s table, checking each line yourself.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 14."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — PRIVACY & POLICY (Chapter 13)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ Minimum data, honest documents, real user control, and Play forms\nI decide         that match the app.\nThe AI does\nProof I demand   ☐ What data and permissions we keep\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Age policy\nRed flags        ☐ Whether to hire a lawyer\nDone when"},{"t":"check","items":["Drafts policy and terms from real facts","Builds consent and privacy settings","Builds in-app and web deletion","Prepares Data safety table","Live policy link","Deletion tested both ways","Data safety matches policy","Breach plan in RUNBOOK.md","Is every line of the policy true today?","Which library collects what?","How does a user withdraw consent?","“We never share data” while using outside services","Pre-ticked consent","Deletion only removes the login","Kids app with no lawyer","Documents live","Forms filled","Deletion and consent tested","Lawyer review if high-risk"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"14","title":"Your admin dashboard","page":98,"sections":[{"id":"14","title":"Introduction","page":98,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your private control room: see users and orders, help customers, stop abuse, and switch features on and off."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: the admin dashboard is the most powerful part of your system, so it is the most valuable target. One weak admin login, or the secret key placed in the admin website, gives an attacker everything, at once. Build it as carefully as a bank vault, not as a quick side project."}]},{"id":"14.1","title":"Do you need one yet?","page":98,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"An admin dashboard is a separate private website (or app) that only you and your team use to manage the live app. Like: the manager’s office at the back of the shop, with the CCTV screens and the safe."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"In the first weeks you may manage everything from the Supabase dashboard itself. Once you have support requests, orders, refunds, abuse or a team, a proper admin tool is safer and faster."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A founder gave a part-time helper full access to the Supabase dashboard to answer support tickets. The helper accidentally deleted a table."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Based on PROJECT_BRIEF.md, list the admin tasks I will need in the first 3 months (for example: find a user, see an order, issue a refund, block an abuser, switch a feature off). For each, say whether I can safely do it in the Supabase dashboard for now, or whether it needs a custom admin page. Recommend the smallest admin tool that covers the rest."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I have a written list of admin tasks.","Nobody except me (the owner) has access to the Supabase dashboard of production.","The admin tool is only as big as the task list."]}]},{"id":"14.2","title":"Where it lives, and the secret key rule","page":99,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"The dashboard is a separate web app (for example on Vercel). Its screens run in the browser. Any action that needs special power runs in server code that the browser cannot see. Like: the manager can press a button in the office, but the safe itself opens only inside the locked back room."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Anything in the browser can be read by whoever opens the page. The Supabase secret (service-role) key ignores all access rules. If it reaches the browser, the whole database is open."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An AI built a fast admin panel by putting the service-role key in the website’s code. Anyone who opened the page source could read and delete all data."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Build the admin dashboard as a separate web app. The browser part may use only the publishable key and the admin’s own login. Any action needing the secret key runs only in server-side code, after checking the admin’s role and second login factor. Search the built website files and prove the secret key is not in them."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Search of the BUILT website files: no secret or service-role key (proof shown).","Every powerful action runs in server code that checks the admin’s role first.","The admin site is on its own address, not linked anywhere public, and blocked from search engines."]}]},{"id":"14.3","title":"Admin login with a second factor (MFA)","page":99,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Admins log in with their password or Google account AND a second proof, usually a 6- digit code from an authenticator app (this is called MFA or 2-step verification). Like: a bank locker that needs your key and the bank’s key together."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Admin passwords get phished and reused. A second factor stops most account takeovers."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An admin reused a password that leaked from another website. The attacker logged in to the admin panel and exported all users."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Require MFA (authenticator app codes) for every admin account. Enforce it on the server: database rules and admin server code must allow admin actions only when the session has passed MFA (Supabase calls this level “aal2”). Add a short session timeout for the admin site. Show me a test where an admin without MFA is refused."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I cannot do any admin action without the authenticator code (I tested).","The “no MFA, no access” test passes.","Admin sessions log out after a short idle time.","My Supabase, GitHub, Google Play and hosting accounts also have 2-step verification on."]}]},{"id":"14.4","title":"Roles: each person gets only what they need","page":100,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Different admin roles with different powers, for example: Owner (everything), Support (look up users and orders, no deletions or refunds above a limit), Viewer (read-only reports). Like: the cashier has the till key, not the safe key; the cleaner has the front-door key, not the till key."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"If a support account is stolen or misused, the damage is limited. It also stops honest mistakes."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Everyone on the team was “admin”. A new support helper issued 200 refunds by mistake."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Create admin roles (Owner, Support, Viewer) stored in the database, not in the app. Write the role rules into ACCESS_MATRIX.md. Enforce them on the server. Write tests that a Support user cannot do Owner actions and a Viewer cannot change anything."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Roles and their powers are written in ACCESS_MATRIX.md.","Role tests pass (Support cannot do Owner actions, Viewer cannot change anything).","Giving and removing a role can only be done by the Owner, and is logged."]}]},{"id":"14.5","title":"The audit log: every admin action recorded","page":101,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A permanent record of every admin action: who, what, when, on which user or order, and why. Like: the CCTV recording and the cash register roll in a shop."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"You can see mistakes and misuse, prove what happened in a dispute, and investigate a breach (Chapter 13)."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A customer’s account was blocked. Nobody knew who did it or why. The customer complained publicly."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Record every admin action in an audit log table: admin, action, target, time, reason, before and after values. Nobody, not even the Owner, can edit or delete log rows through the dashboard. Show the log in the dashboard with filters. Test that changing a log row is refused."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every admin action creates a log row (I checked after doing three actions).","Editing or deleting a log row is refused (test shown).","Dangerous actions require a written reason."]}]},{"id":"14.6","title":"Safe handling of dangerous actions","page":101,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Extra protection on actions that cannot easily be undone: refunds, deleting users, changing prices, bulk actions, changing roles. Like: a bank asking for a second signature on large cheques."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Most admin disasters are honest mistakes made quickly."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A “delete all test users” button deleted real users because the filter was wrong."},{"t":"prompt","text":"For dangerous actions: show exactly what will change and how many records, require typing a confirmation word, require a reason, set limits (for example max refund per day for Support), and prefer “disable” over “delete”. No bulk delete without a backup taken first. Show me each dangerous action and its protection."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every dangerous action shows a preview and needs confirmation.","Support limits exist and are tested.","There is no one-click bulk delete."]}]},{"id":"14.7","title":"Showing personal data carefully","page":102,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Admins see only the personal data needed for the task, often partly hidden (masked), with full details shown only on request and logged."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a delivery person sees the address, not the customer’s bank statement."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Fewer eyes on personal data means fewer leaks, and it follows the “minimum data” principle of DPDP."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The user list showed every phone number and address on one page. A screenshot of it was shared in a team chat and leaked."},{"t":"prompt","text":"In lists, show masked phone numbers and emails (e.g. 98••••12). Full details appear only on a single user’s page, for roles that need them, and viewing them is logged. Do not add any “export all users” button without my approval."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Lists show masked personal data.","Viewing full details is logged.","No bulk export exists unless I approved it, and it is Owner-only and logged."]}]},{"id":"14.8","title":"What a good admin dashboard contains","page":102,"blocks":[{"t":"pre","text":"Section           What it lets you do                                 Who\n                                                                      Owner, Support\nUsers             Find a user, see status, block or unblock, handle\n                  deletion requests                                   Owner, Support\n                                                                      Owner (Support with\nOrders / content  See orders or posts, change status, handle reports  limits)\nRefunds           Issue refunds within limits, with reason\nSection    What it lets you do                                Who\nAbuse                                                         Owner, Support\n           Suspicious sign-ups, OTP use, promo use, reported\nSwitches   content (Chapter 9)                                Owner only\nHealth     Kill switches, minimum app version, maintenance    Owner, Viewer\n           message (Chapter 9)\nAudit log                                                     Owner\nTeam       Errors, sign-ups, orders, costs today vs normal    Owner only\n           (Chapter 16)"},{"t":"h","text":"Every admin action, filterable"},{"t":"h","text":"Add or remove admins and roles"}]},{"id":"14.9","title":"When someone leaves the team","page":103,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A checklist to remove access the same day a helper, freelancer or employee stops working with you."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: collecting the shop keys and changing the locker code when staff leave."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Old accounts that still work are a common way in for attackers and for unhappy ex-staff."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A freelancer who built the admin panel still had owner access a year later. Their account was hacked."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Write an “access removal” checklist in RUNBOOK.md covering: admin dashboard role, Supabase, GitHub, Play Console, hosting, payment gateway, SMS provider, email, and any shared passwords or keys to rotate."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The checklist exists in RUNBOOK.md.","I review the list of people with access every month (Chapter 17)."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"An admin dashboard holds the keys to everything. For apps with money or many users, include it in the human security review (Chapter 8). Never give anyone production access “just for today”."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Write your admin task list (14.1) and start with the smallest tool.","Turn on 2-step verification on every account you own today.","Demand the secret-key search and the MFA and role tests before using the dashboard live.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 15."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — ADMIN DASHBOARD (Chapter 14)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ A small, private control room with strong login, limited roles and a full\nI decide         record of actions.\nThe AI does\nProof I demand   ☐ Which admin tasks are needed\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Who gets which role\nRed flags        ☐ Limits on refunds and dangerous actions\nDone when        ☐ Builds a separate admin web app"},{"t":"check","items":["Keeps the secret key server-side only","Enforces MFA and roles on the server","Builds the audit log","Built-site search: no secret key","No-MFA and role tests","Audit log rows after my test actions","Masked personal data in lists","Where does the secret key live?","What can a Support user NOT do?","Who did this action, and why?","Secret key in browser code","No MFA for admins","Everyone is Owner","Editable or missing audit log","One-click bulk delete","All admin tests pass","MFA on everywhere","Access-removal checklist written"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"15","title":"Release: getting onto Google Play safely","page":106,"sections":[{"id":"15","title":"Introduction","page":106,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Big companies never release to everyone at once. They release to a few, watch, and then widen the circle."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: with a new personal Play developer account, you cannot publish on day one. Google requires a closed test with real testers for two weeks first. Plan this into your launch date now, and start finding your testers early."},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"For personal developer accounts created after 13 November 2023, Google Play requires a closed test with at least 12 testers opted in continuously for at least 14 days before you can apply for production access. Organisation accounts are treated differently. Android is also rolling out developer identity verification, starting in some countries from September 2026 and globally in 2027. Where to check: Play Console Help → “App testing requirements for new personal developer accounts”, and developer.android.com/developer-verification. Read on 22 Nov 2026."}]},{"id":"15.1","title":"Build types: debug versus release","page":106,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A debug build is for testing: it shows extra information and connects to the development backend. A release build is what users get: optimised, locked down, and connected to production."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: a rehearsal costume with safety pins showing, versus the finished outfit on stage."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"A debug build shipped by mistake leaks information, runs slowly, and may point to your test database."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A release was built with debug settings. Logs printed tokens, and the app was connected to the development database. Real orders went to the test system."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Make the release build: not debuggable, R8 shrinking and obfuscation on, no debug logs, connected only to production, with the DEV label removed. Show me a checklist proving each setting for the exact build I am about to upload, and its version number."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The release checklist shows each setting for THIS build.","The app shows no DEV label.","The version number is higher than the last release.","It is an Android App Bundle (.aab), which Google Play requires for new apps."]}]},{"id":"15.2","title":"Signing and key safety","page":107,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Every Android app is “signed” with a digital key, like a seal on an envelope. With Play App Signing, Google keeps the main app signing key safe, and you keep a separate upload key that you use to send new versions to Google. Like: the bank keeps the master key to your locker; you keep a key to the bank’s front desk. If you lose yours, the bank can issue a new one after checking your identity."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Without the right key, you cannot update your app. If your key is stolen, someone could try to upload fake updates."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Google sign-in worked perfectly in testing but failed for every user from the Play Store, because only the debug key’s fingerprint was registered with Google, not the Play app signing key’s."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Use Play App Signing. Create an upload key and tell me exactly where it is and its password, so I can store both in my password manager and one offline backup. Never put the key or its password in the project or GitHub. Register the Play app signing key’s SHA fingerprints with every service that needs them (Google sign-in, etc.) and show me where."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Play App Signing is on (Play Console → App integrity).","The upload key file and password are in my password manager AND one offline backup.","The key is NOT in the project or GitHub (search shown).","Google sign-in works on a build installed from the Play testing track, not only from the computer."]},{"t":"label","text":"IF YOU LOSE THE UPLOAD KEY"},{"t":"p","text":"With Play App Signing, the account owner can request an upload key reset in Play Console. It takes some time, so keep backups and avoid needing it."}]},{"id":"15.3","title":"Your Play Console account","page":108,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"The Google account and developer profile that owns your apps. It can be a personal account or an organisation account. Like: the registered owner of the shop. Whoever controls this account controls the business."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"If this account is lost or hacked, you can lose all your apps. Google also checks developer identity."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The Play account was created with the freelance developer’s email. When they left, the owner could not access their own app."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Do not use your own accounts for my app. Explain to me, step by step, how I set up my own Play Console account, add you or a helper with the smallest permissions needed, and protect it."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The Play Console account is owned by MY email, with 2-step verification on.","Helpers have their own logins with limited permissions, never my password.","My developer identity and contact details are verified and up to date.","I know whether I have a personal or organisation account, and what that means for testing."]}]},{"id":"15.4","title":"Store listing and app content forms","page":108,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Your app’s page on Google Play (name, description, screenshots, icon) and the “App content” forms (content rating, target audience, ads, Data safety, privacy policy, account deletion). Like: the shop front and the licences on the wall."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"An honest, clear listing brings the right users. Wrong or misleading forms get the app rejected or removed."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Screenshots showed features that were “coming soon”. Reviews called the app fake, and Google flagged the listing as misleading."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Draft my store listing: short and full description in simple English, honest feature list, and a plan for 4–8 screenshots taken from the real release build. List every App content form I must complete in Play Console, with the answers prepared from our project files. Mark anything I must decide myself."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every claim and screenshot matches the real app today.","Content rating, target audience, ads, Data safety, privacy policy and deletion forms are complete.","I filled the forms myself and checked each answer."]}]},{"id":"15.5","title":"Testing tracks: internal, closed, open","page":109,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Play Console lets you release to small groups first. Internal testing: you and a few people, fast. Closed testing: invited testers. Open testing: anyone can join. Then production. Like: a soft opening for friends, then a trial week for invited guests, then the grand opening."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Each step catches problems with fewer people affected. Google also runs a free “pre- launch report” on real test phones."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"The app went straight to everyone. A crash on one popular phone brand hit thousands of users in the first hour."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Plan my testing tracks: internal first (me plus 2–3 people), then closed testing with at least the number of testers Google currently requires, for the required days. Write a simple tester guide: what to test, how to report problems. Check the pre-launch report after every upload and summarise it for me in plain words."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Internal test done and my 20-minute real-phone test passed (Chapter 12).","Closed test ran with enough real testers for the required days.","Pre-launch report reviewed: no crashes, no serious warnings.","Tester feedback is written in PROJECT_LOG.md and handled."]}]},{"id":"15.6","title":"Staged rollout: 1%, 10%, 50%, 100%","page":110,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"For updates, Play lets you release to a percentage of users first. You raise the percentage step by step, and you can halt it if something goes wrong. Like: opening one billing counter first on a busy day, then more as they run smoothly."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"If a new version has a bug, only a few users see it, and you can stop it before everyone gets it."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An update that crashed on Android 12 went to 100% of users at once. Ratings dropped from 4.5 to 3.1 in two days."},{"t":"prompt","text":"For this update, plan a staged rollout: 1% → 10% → 50% → 100%. For each step, tell me what to check before going up (crash rate, ANR rate, error alerts, reviews, support messages, key business numbers) and the minimum waiting time. Write the halt steps in RUNBOOK.md."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I used the gates table below at every step.","I know exactly where the “Halt rollout” button is.","Rollout percentages do not increase automatically; I raise each step myself."]},{"t":"label","text":"NOTE"},{"t":"p","text":"Staged rollout works for updates. The very first production release of a new app cannot be staged. That is why closed testing (15.5) matters so much for the first launch."},{"t":"h","text":"Rollout gates"},{"t":"pre","text":"Step  Wait at least  Go up only if                  Halt if\n1%    1–2 days       No new crash types; crash and ANR Any new crash in a main flow,"},{"t":"p","text":"rates normal; no error alerts  or payment/login errors"},{"t":"pre","text":"10%   2 days         Same as above; reviews and support Crash or ANR rate rising;\n                     messages normal                complaints about the same"},{"t":"p","text":"problem"},{"t":"pre","text":"50%   2–3 days       Same; business numbers (sign-ups, Any drop in orders or sign-ups\n                     orders) normal                 you cannot explain\nStep  Wait at least  Go up only if              Halt if\n100%  —              Everything above is clean\n                                                Halt and use kill switch / force-\n                                                update if needed"}]},{"id":"15.7","title":"Version numbers and release notes","page":111,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Every release has a version number that always goes up, and short notes saying what changed."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: the edition number on a printed book."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Version numbers let you know which users have which version, link crashes to releases, and use force-update (Chapter 9)."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Two builds had the same name. A crash report could not be matched to the right code."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Increase the version code and version name for this release. Tag the save point in Git with the version. Write user-facing release notes in simple English, and an internal note in PROJECT_LOG.md listing every change and migration in this release."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Version code is higher than the last release.","The Git save point is tagged with the version.","Release notes are honest and simple."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"Google’s review can still reject or delay an app, sometimes without much explanation. Leave buffer time before any launch date you announce, and never promise users a launch day until production access is approved."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Start recruiting closed testers now (friends, customers, communities).","Store your upload key and password in two safe places today.","Go through Chapter 22’s “before launch” page before applying for production.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 16."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — RELEASE (Chapter 15)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ A correctly built, signed, honestly listed app reaches users in safe\nI decide         steps.\nThe AI does\nProof I demand   ☐ Launch timing\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Who the testers are\nRed flags        ☐ When to raise or halt each rollout step\nDone when"},{"t":"check","items":["Prepares the release build and checklist","Drafts listing and form answers","Summarises pre-launch reports","Writes release notes","Release build checklist","Google sign-in works from a Play build","Pre-launch report clean","Gate checks at each rollout step","Is this exact build release-ready?","Where is the upload key backed up?","What would make us halt?","Debug build uploaded","Keys in GitHub","Screenshots of features that do not exist","Going straight to 100%","Closed test passed","Production approved","Rollout reached 100% with clean gates"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"16","title":"Running it live","page":114,"sections":[{"id":"16","title":"Introduction","page":114,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Launch is not the finish line. It is the moment real problems start arriving. You need eyes, alarms and a plan."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: most users never report a problem. They just uninstall. If you only learn about bugs from reviews, you are already late. Your app must tell YOU when something is wrong, before users do."}]},{"id":"16.1","title":"Crash reports","page":114,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A crash-reporting tool (for example Firebase Crashlytics or Sentry) sends a report every time the app crashes or freezes on any user’s phone, grouped by cause. Like: a smoke detector in every room of your building, wired to your phone."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"You see crashes within minutes, how many users are affected, on which phones and app versions, and which line of code failed."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A crash on one phone brand affected 15% of users for three weeks. Nobody knew, because there was no crash reporting."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Add crash reporting to the release build. Do not send personal data (names, phone numbers, emails, addresses, tokens) in crash reports or logs. Tag reports with app version. Show me a test crash arriving in the dashboard, and list what data it contains. Add the tool to DEPENDENCIES.md and the Data safety answers."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["A test crash appeared in the crash dashboard.","Reports contain no personal data (I looked at one).","The tool is listed in the privacy policy and Data safety form."]}]},{"id":"16.2","title":"Monitoring: the health of the whole system","page":114,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Watching key numbers every day: errors on the backend, slow database queries, sign- ups, orders, costs, and Android vitals in Play Console. Like: the dashboard of a car: speed, fuel, engine temperature. You glance at it all the time."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Problems often show up first as a strange number: orders dropping to zero, errors climbing, costs jumping."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A database rule change blocked new orders. Nothing crashed, so no alarm rang. Orders were zero for a whole day."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Create a simple “Health” page in my admin dashboard showing today versus a normal day: sign-ups, logins, main actions (orders/posts), backend errors, OTPs sent, storage used, and estimated cost. Also tell me which Supabase and Play Console pages to check weekly."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The Health page shows today vs normal.","I know which numbers are normal for my app.","I check it daily in the first two weeks after launch."]}]},{"id":"16.3","title":"Alerts to your phone","page":115,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Automatic messages (email, push, chat) when a number crosses a danger line. Like: a burglar alarm that calls you, instead of a camera you must watch all night."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"You cannot watch dashboards all day. Alerts wake you up only when it matters."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An OTP attack ran all night. The dashboard showed it clearly, but nobody was looking. The bill arrived in the morning."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Set up alerts that reach my phone for: new crash types or crash spikes, backend error spikes, OTPs or sign-ups far above normal, main actions dropping to zero, costs above my daily budget, and any security advisor warnings. Send me a test alert for each."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I received a test alert for every alert type.","Alert limits are written in RUNBOOK.md.","Alerts are not so frequent that I start ignoring them."]}]},{"id":"16.4","title":"Privacy-safe analytics","page":116,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Counting how the app is used (which screens, which features, where users drop off) to make better decisions, without spying on people. Like: counting how many customers enter each aisle, not following individuals around the shop."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"You need to know what works. But heavy tracking adds legal duties, Data safety entries and user distrust."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An analytics library recorded every screen with the user’s email attached and shared data for advertising. The Data safety form did not mention it."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Propose the smallest set of usage events that answer my 5 most important business questions. No personal data in events, no advertising sharing, and respect the user’s consent choice. Update the privacy policy and Data safety answers to match. Show me the list of events."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I have a written list of events, each linked to a business question.","No personal data in any event.","Analytics follows the user’s consent choice.","Policy and Data safety form updated."]}]},{"id":"16.5","title":"The incident plan: when something breaks","page":116,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A written, step-by-step plan for live problems: how bad is it, who does what, how to stop the damage, how to tell users, how to learn from it. Like: a hospital’s emergency procedure: triage first, treatment second, paperwork after."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"In a crisis, people panic and make it worse. A plan written on a calm day saves hours."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Payments were failing. The owner asked the AI to “fix it fast” directly on production. The fix broke login too."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Write an incident section in RUNBOOK.md with: severity levels (critical: money, data leak, nobody can log in; high: a main feature broken; low: cosmetic), the first 15 minutes checklist, which kill switch to use for which problem, how to halt a rollout, how to message users, and a template for a short “what happened and what we changed” note afterwards."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["RUNBOOK.md has severity levels and a first-15-minutes list.","I did a practice drill once (pretend payments are failing).","Every incident gets a short written note afterwards in PROJECT_LOG.md."]},{"t":"h","text":"The first 15 minutes of any serious problem"},{"t":"p","text":"1. Breathe. Do not let the AI change production code in a hurry."},{"t":"p","text":"2. Check alerts, the crash dashboard and the Health page: what exactly is broken, since when, for how many users?"},{"t":"p","text":"3. If money or data is at risk: use the kill switch for that feature now."},{"t":"p","text":"4. If a new release caused it: halt the rollout in Play Console."},{"t":"p","text":"5. If a data leak is possible: follow the breach plan (Chapter 13) and start the 72-hour clock."},{"t":"p","text":"6. Tell affected users briefly and honestly if they are affected."},{"t":"p","text":"7. Then, in development, find the cause and fix it with a test, and release the fix through the normal steps."}]},{"id":"16.6","title":"Rollback: undoing a bad change","page":117,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Going back to the last good state. For the app: halt the rollout and release a fixed version. For the backend: undo the database change or restore from backup. For features: switch them off. Like: pressing “undo”, but knowing that some things (like a sent SMS) cannot be undone."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"You cannot remove an app version from phones that already installed it. That is why kill switches and force-update exist, and why database changes must be backwards- compatible."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A database change was rolled back, but the new app version still expected the new structure. Both old and new apps broke."},{"t":"prompt","text":"For every release, write in PROJECT_LOG.md how to roll it back: app (halt + kill switch + force-update if needed), database (reverse migration or restore point), settings. Database changes must keep working with the previous app version for at least one release."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Every release has a written rollback plan.","Database changes are backwards-compatible with the previous app version.","I have tried a rollback once in staging."]}]},{"id":"16.7","title":"Feature flags: switching features on gradually","page":118,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Settings that turn features on or off for some or all users without a new app version (the same system as the kill switch in Chapter 9). Like: dimmer switches instead of plain on/off switches."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"You can ship a new feature switched off, turn it on for staff first, then 10% of users, then everyone, and turn it off instantly if it misbehaves."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A new checkout was released to all users at once. It had a bug. The only fix was a new app version, which took two days to reach users."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Put every new risky feature behind a feature flag that defaults to OFF, controlled from the admin dashboard. Allow turning it on for admins only, then a percentage of users, then everyone. Remove old flags once a feature is stable."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["New risky features ship switched off.","I can turn a feature on for myself only, then for more users.","Old flags are cleaned up monthly (Chapter 17)."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"No monitoring catches everything. Some problems only appear in user complaints. Read every review and support message in the first month, reply politely, and turn repeated complaints into tests."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Confirm crash reports, the Health page and phone alerts all work before launch.","Write and practise the incident plan once.","Check the Health page daily for the first two weeks after launch.","Fill in the Driver Card, then go to Chapter 17."]},{"t":"p","text":"DRIVER CARD — RUNNING IT LIVE (Chapter 16)"},{"t":"p","text":"Tear-out page. Keep it beside you while you work on this stage."},{"t":"pre","text":"Goal             ☐ You hear about problems before users do, and you can stop damage\nI decide         in minutes.\nThe AI does\nProof I demand   ☐ Alert limits\nQuestions I ask  ☐ Which events to count\nRed flags        ☐ Severity of each incident\nDone when"},{"t":"check","items":["Sets up crash reporting and alerts","Builds the Health page","Writes the incident and rollback plans","Builds feature flags","Test crash received","Test alerts on my phone","Practice drill done","Rollback plan per release","What is broken, since when, for how many?","Which switch stops the damage?","How do we undo this release?","No crash reporting","Personal data in logs or analytics","Fixing directly on production in a panic","Alerts nobody receives","Alerts tested","Incident plan practised","First two weeks watched daily"]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"17","title":"Keeping it healthy","page":121,"sections":[{"id":"17","title":"Introduction","page":121,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Apps rot if nobody looks after them. Libraries get old, rules change, costs creep up, and old access is forgotten."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: an app you stop maintaining becomes unsafe and eventually disappears from Google Play for new users. Every year Google raises the required target API level. Plan a few hours every month and a bigger check every year, forever, for every live app."},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"Google Play raises the target API level requirement every year, usually with a deadline around 31 August. Apps that fall too far behind stop being shown to new users on newer phones, and updates are blocked until fixed. Where to check: Play Console Help → “Target API level requirements for Google Play apps”. Read on 22 Nov 2026."}]},{"id":"17.1","title":"Weekly (15 minutes)","page":121,"blocks":[{"t":"check","items":["Look at the Health page: anything unusual this week?","Crash dashboard: any new crash types? Crash and ANR rates below Google’s lines?","Read new reviews and support messages. Reply. Note repeated problems.","Check cost dashboards: spending on track?","Fill in the Weekly post-launch check sheet from the print pack."]}]},{"id":"17.2","title":"Monthly (1–2 hours)","page":121,"blocks":[{"t":"check","items":["Security updates: ask the AI to list libraries with known security problems"]},{"t":"p","text":"(Dependabot alerts) and update them with tests."},{"t":"check","items":["Run the security self-audit prompt from Chapter 8 and fix anything high.","Review who has access to everything: Play Console, Supabase, GitHub, admin dashboard, payment and SMS providers. Remove anyone who no longer needs it.","Check Supabase Security Advisor and Performance Advisor.","Review abuse numbers (OTPs, sign-ups, promo use). Adjust limits if needed.","Clean up old feature flags and finished TODOs.","Check backups are running.","Update PROJECT_LOG.md with a monthly summary."]}]},{"id":"17.3","title":"Every 3 months","page":122,"blocks":[{"t":"check","items":["Practice restore: restore a backup into a separate project and check the data"]},{"t":"p","text":"(Chapter 6)."},{"t":"check","items":["Practice incident drill (Chapter 16).","Re-check privacy policy, Data safety form and DATA_MAP.md still match the app.","Speed check: re-measure the SPEED_REPORT.md numbers on a cheap phone.","Run the hostile second-AI audit in a fresh chat (Chapter 19)."]}]},{"id":"17.4","title":"Every year","page":122,"blocks":[{"t":"check","items":["Target API level: update to the new required level before Google’s deadline, with full"]},{"t":"pre","text":"⏰ testing.      CHECK THE DATE box in this book against the official sources."},{"t":"check","items":["Re-check every","Review all costs and plans: are you on the right plan? Any service you no longer use?","Rotate important keys and passwords (backend secrets, payment, SMS).","Review the threat model: new features, new risks?","Renew your domain and check the privacy policy and deletion pages still load.","Consider a human security review if the app has grown."]}]},{"id":"17.5","title":"Updates without breaking things","page":122,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Updating libraries, Android versions and backend versions in small, tested steps."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: servicing a car one part at a time, testing it on the road after each."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Old versions have known security holes. But updating everything at once often breaks things."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"Twenty libraries were updated in one go a week before a festival sale. Three broke, and nobody knew which."},{"t":"prompt","text":"List all outdated libraries and tools, grouped by risk (security fixes first). Update one group at a time on a separate branch, run all tests and CI, and give me a change report per group. Never update right before a big business event."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["Security updates done first.","One group per save point, CI green after each.","Release went through staged rollout."]}]},{"id":"17.6","title":"Cost review","page":123,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Comparing what you pay each month with what you expected and what you actually use. Like: checking the electricity bill and switching off lights in empty rooms."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Costs grow silently: bigger plans, forgotten add-ons, unused services, abuse."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"An unused test project on a paid plan cost money every month for a year."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Make a cost table for this month: every service, plan, monthly cost, what we use it for, and usage versus limits. Highlight anything unused, oversized, or growing faster than users. Suggest savings with their risks."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I know my total monthly cost and cost per active user.","Unused services and projects are closed.","Spend caps and alerts are still on."]},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"Maintenance is not exciting, and the AI will not remind you to do it. Put these routines in your phone calendar today, as recurring reminders. An unmaintained app is a slowly growing risk."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Put weekly, monthly, 3-monthly and yearly reminders in your phone calendar now.","Print a stack of Weekly check sheets from the print pack.","Go to Chapter 18 to set up your project memory files."]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"18","title":"MCP, skills, plugins and project memory files","page":124,"sections":[{"id":"18","title":"Introduction","page":124,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The add-ons that make the AI more capable, and the written files that make it remember. Both need rules."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: every add-on that lets the AI DO more also lets mistakes and attacks do more. An AI connected to your live database can be tricked by text hidden inside that data. Give the AI the smallest access that does the job, and never give it your production data by default."},{"t":"h","text":"The four add-ons in one table"},{"t":"pre","text":"Add-on                Plain meaning                  Picture           Main risk"},{"t":"p","text":"Project memory files  Written notes the AI reads The shift notebook Wrong or old notes (CLAUDE.md etc.)"},{"t":"pre","text":"                      at the start of every session in a kitchen       repeat mistakes\nSkills                Saved instructions for a kind Recipe cards in a A bad recipe card\n                      of task, used when needed drawer                 teaches bad habits\nMCP servers           Connectors that let the AI     Extension cables  The AI can read or\n                                                                       change things in those\n                      use outside services           into other rooms  rooms"},{"t":"p","text":"(database, GitHub, browser)"},{"t":"pre","text":"Plugins               A package that bundles         A toolbox         You install everything\n                      skills, connectors and         someone else      inside, including things\n                      settings to install in one go  packed            you did not check"}]},{"id":"18.1","title":"MCP (Model Context Protocol)","page":124,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"MCP is a standard way to plug outside tools into an AI. With a Supabase MCP server, the AI can read your database structure, run queries and check advisors. With a GitHub MCP, it can read issues and pull requests."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: giving a helper a key card to another office. Useful, but they can now open that office’s drawers."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Used well, MCP saves you copying and pasting, and lets the AI check real settings instead of guessing. Used carelessly, it lets the AI (or an attacker who tricks it) touch real data."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A builder connected the AI to the production database with full access. A customer had typed hidden instructions into a support message field. When the AI read that row, it followed the instructions and copied private data into a public place."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Connect the Supabase MCP server only to the DEVELOPMENT project, in read-only mode and scoped to that single project, with only the tool groups we need. Never connect it to production. Keep manual approval on for every action. Show me the exact connection settings."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["MCP points to the development project only (I saw the project name).","Read-only and project-scoped are switched on.","I approve each action; “always approve” is OFF.","Every connected MCP server is listed in CLAUDE.md with its access level."]},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ CHECK THE DATE"},{"t":"p","text":"Supabase’s own MCP guide says to use it with non-production data, recommends read-only and project-scoped mode, and warns about prompt injection (hidden instructions inside data). Where to check: supabase.com/docs/guides/ai-tools/mcp (security section). Read on 22 Nov 2026."},{"t":"h","text":"MCP servers worth considering for an Android builder"},{"t":"pre","text":"MCP server          Useful for                         Safe setup\nSupabase            Checking tables, rules, advisors,  Development project only, read-\n                    logs                               only, project-scoped\nGitHub              Reading issues, save points, CI    Access only to this one repository\n                    results\nBrowser / web docs  Reading official documentation     Read-only; never log in to your\n                                                       accounts through it\nOthers (payments,   Rarely needed while building       Only test-mode keys; never live\nemail, analytics)                                      payment keys"}]},{"id":"18.2","title":"Skills","page":125,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A skill is a saved instruction file for a kind of task (for example “how we write database rules” or “our release checklist”). The AI loads it only when that task comes up."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: laminated recipe cards: the cook pulls out the right card only when making that dish."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Skills make the AI follow YOUR way of working every time, without filling every chat with long instructions."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A skill downloaded from the internet told the AI to “disable RLS while developing for speed”. Every project that used it shipped with open tables."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Turn this handbook’s checklists into skills for my project: one for the release checklist, one for the security self-audit, one for the edge-case sweep, and one for the change report. Show me each skill’s full text before saving it."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I read every skill’s text before it was saved.","No skill tells the AI to skip, disable or bypass anything.","Skills from other people are only used after I (or a fresh AI) reviewed them."]}]},{"id":"18.3","title":"Plugins","page":126,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"A plugin is a ready-made bundle that can include skills, MCP connectors, automatic actions (hooks) and helper agents. You install it once. Like: buying a pre-packed toolbox. Handy, but you should look inside before using every tool on your car."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Plugins can save a lot of setup. They can also quietly add connectors and automatic actions with more access than you want."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"A plugin added an automatic action that sent project files to an outside service for “analysis”. Nobody had read what was inside."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Before installing any plugin, list everything inside it: skills, MCP servers, hooks (automatic actions) and agents, what each can access, and where any data is sent. Tell me if it comes from an official or well-known source. Do not install until I say yes."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["I saw the full list of what the plugin contains.","It comes from an official or well-known publisher.","Nothing in it sends data to services I did not approve.","Installed plugins are listed in CLAUDE.md."]}]},{"id":"18.4","title":"Project memory files","page":127,"blocks":[{"t":"label","text":"WHAT IT IS"},{"t":"p","text":"Plain text files (ending in .md) inside your project that hold the decisions, rules and current status. The AI reads them at the start of each session, so it does not forget what was agreed."},{"t":"p","text":"Like: the shift notebook in a hospital ward: every nurse reads it when starting, and writes in it before leaving."},{"t":"label","text":"WHY IT MATTERS TO YOUR USERS AND YOUR MONEY"},{"t":"p","text":"Chats are forgotten. Files are not. These files are how you stay the driver across months, multiple chats and even different AI tools."},{"t":"label","text":"WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU IGNORE IT"},{"t":"p","text":"All decisions lived in old chats. A new chat rebuilt the login differently, undid a security rule, and brought back a removed feature."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Create the project memory files listed in my handbook (CLAUDE.md, PROJECT_BRIEF.md, PROJECT_LOG.md, TODO_NEXT.md, GLOSSARY.md) using the templates I give you. Read them at the start of every session. Update PROJECT_LOG.md and TODO_NEXT.md at the end of every session. Keep CLAUDE.md short: rules only, no long history."},{"t":"label","text":"HOW YOU CHECK THE AI DID IT (PROOF TO DEMAND)"},{"t":"check","items":["The files exist in the project and on GitHub.","PROJECT_LOG.md gets a dated entry every session.","CLAUDE.md is short enough to read in 3 minutes.","When I start a new chat, the AI summarises the key rules correctly."]},{"t":"h","text":"Your control files and what each is for"},{"t":"pre","text":"File              What it holds                                      Created in\nCLAUDE.md                                                            Ch 18\nPROJECT_BRIEF.md  The house rules the AI must always follow (short)  Ch 3\nPROJECT_LOG.md    Promise, users, MUST features, user stories, “not  Every session\n                  in this version”"},{"t":"p","text":"Dated diary of decisions, changes, releases, incidents"},{"t":"pre","text":"File                    What it holds                                     Created in\nTODO_NEXT.md                                                              Every session\n                        The next small steps, and every shortcut or todo\nGLOSSARY.md             left behind                                       Ch 18,\n                                                                          ongoing\n                        Words used in this project, in plain language     Ch 4\n                                                                          Ch 5\nDESIGN_SYSTEM.md        Colours, text sizes, spacing, components          Ch 6\nARCHITECTURE.md         Parts, services, where each key lives, exit plan\nDATA_MAP.md /           What data we keep; who may do what                Ch 8\nACCESS_MATRIX.md\nTHREAT_MODEL.md /       Threats and locks; every library and why          Ch 9\nDEPENDENCIES.md                                                           Ch 10\nABUSE_LIMITS.md         Every limit and its test                          Ch 11\nSPEED_REPORT.md         Before-and-after speed numbers                    Ch 6, 9, 13,\nEDGE_CASES.md           Every edge case, status and test                  16\nRUNBOOK.md              Restore, incident, breach, kill switch, access    Ch 12\n                        removal steps\nAUDIT_REPORT_[date].md  Findings from each fresh-AI audit"},{"t":"p","text":"Starter CLAUDE.md (copy and fill in)"},{"t":"label","text":"CLAUDE.MD TEMPLATE"},{"t":"p","text":"# House rules for this project Owner: [MY NAME]. I am not technical. Explain everything in plain English. App: [ONE-SENTENCE PROMISE]. Details: PROJECT_BRIEF.md. Start of every session: read CLAUDE.md, PROJECT_BRIEF.md, PROJECT_LOG.md, TODO_NEXT.md. Summarise in 5 lines. End of every session: update PROJECT_LOG.md and TODO_NEXT.md. Make a save point. Work in small steps. Save point before and after each step. Never touch production (database, keys, Play Console) without my written OK in this chat. Never put secrets in the app or in Git. Only the publishable key may be in the app. Never disable, skip or bypass a security rule, test or check. If stuck, stop and tell me. Never delete or weaken a test to make it pass. Never add a library, service or paid tool without asking me. Every fact about rules, prices or versions needs an official link. Done means proof: test counts (passed/failed/skipped), screenshots, or a build I can install. Connected MCP servers: [LIST, with access level]. Installed plugins: [LIST]."},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"Memory files only help if they are true and current. An old CLAUDE.md that says the wrong thing is worse than none. Re-read it every month (Chapter 17) and correct it whenever you correct the AI twice for the same thing."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Create CLAUDE.md from the template for your current project today.","Check every MCP connection: development only, read-only, project-scoped.","List every installed plugin and remove any you have not checked.","Go to Chapter 19 and copy the prompts you will use most."]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"19","title":"My prompt library","page":130,"sections":[{"id":"19","title":"Introduction","page":130,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ready-to-copy prompts for the moments that matter. Replace anything in [SQUARE BRACKETS]. Tip: copy these into a notes app on your computer so you can paste them quickly. The 6 questions (Chapter 0) and the chapter prompts are the rest of your library."}]},{"id":"19.1","title":"Start a new project","page":130,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use when: you begin a brand-new app."},{"t":"prompt","text":"I am starting a new Android app. I am not technical; I make decisions, you build. Explain everything in plain English. Stack: Kotlin + Jetpack Compose, Supabase (dev and prod projects), Supabase Auth, GitHub private repo. First, create CLAUDE.md, PROJECT_BRIEF.md, PROJECT_LOG.md, TODO_NEXT.md and GLOSSARY.md from my templates. Then act as a strict investor and challenge my idea: [IDEA]. Do not write app code yet."}]},{"id":"19.2","title":"Start a stage","page":130,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use when: you move to the next stage of the journey (Chapter 2)."},{"t":"prompt","text":"We are starting stage [NUMBER + NAME]. Read the project files first and summarise the decisions that limit this stage. Tell me: what “done” means for this stage, what you need from me, the risks, and the list of small steps. Do step 1 only. Then stop and tell me how to check it."}]},{"id":"19.3","title":"Start and end of every session","page":131,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use when: every time you open or close a working session."},{"t":"prompt","text":"START: Read CLAUDE.md, PROJECT_BRIEF.md, PROJECT_LOG.md and TODO_NEXT.md. Summarise in 5 lines: where we are, the rules that matter today, and the next step.\nEND: Update PROJECT_LOG.md (what changed, decisions, problems) and TODO_NEXT.md (next steps, every shortcut or todo left). Make a save point. Give me the change report."}]},{"id":"19.4","title":"Demand proof","page":131,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use when: the AI says “done”, “fixed” or “working”."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Before I accept this, show me proof:\n1) Test results with counts: passed, failed, skipped.\n2) Which tests you added, changed or removed, and why.\n3) A screenshot or short screen recording of it working, including the error and offline cases.\n4) Any security rule, setting or permission you changed.\n5) Anything temporary or unfinished. If you cannot prove it, say “not done”."}]},{"id":"19.5","title":"Audit an existing app","page":131,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use when: you have an app already built and want to know its real state."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Audit this app as a senior engineer joining a new company. Do not change anything. Using my handbook’s checklists, give me a plain-English report on: security (OWASP MASVS groups), database rules and backups, secrets, abuse limits, speed, edge cases, tests, privacy and Play policy, release setup, monitoring. For each area: status (good / weak / missing), proof (file, setting, test or search result), and risk if ignored. End with the top 10 fixes ranked by damage, and a suggested order."}]},{"id":"19.6","title":"Fix speed","page":132,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use when: the app feels slow or laggy."},{"t":"prompt","text":"The app feels slow when [WHAT YOU NOTICED]. Do not change code yet. First measure on a low-end phone profile and slow network: cold start, slow frames on [SCREEN], memory, app size. Save to SPEED_REPORT.md. Then list the likely causes with evidence, ranked by impact. Fix only the top one, measure again, and show before and after."}]},{"id":"19.7","title":"Harden security","page":132,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use when: before a release, or after adding a feature that touches data or money."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Act as a hostile security tester. Check this app and backend against OWASP MASVS v2 and the OWASP Mobile Top 10 (2024). Specifically try: user B reading or changing user A’s data in every table and bucket; changing IDs; normal user doing admin actions; secrets in the built app; prices or rewards trusted from the app; missing limits on OTP, sign-up, promos, uploads, AI calls. Report what worked as an attack, with proof. Then propose fixes in order of damage. Do not fix until I approve."}]},{"id":"19.8","title":"When I feel stuck","page":132,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use when: you do not know what to do next, or the AI is going in circles."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Stop. Do not change any code. In plain English: where are we, what is working, what is broken, and what have we tried? Go back to the last save point where things worked, if needed. Give me 3 options for what to do next, with the risk and effort of each, and your recommendation."}]},{"id":"19.9","title":"Undo a change","page":133,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use when: something broke after a change."},{"t":"prompt","text":"The last change broke [WHAT]. Show me the save points from today with their messages. Tell me which save point was the last good one and what will be lost if we go back to it. Wait for my OK. Then go back safely without deleting history, and confirm the app works again. If the database was changed too, tell me the rollback plan before doing anything."}]},{"id":"19.10","title":"Hand off to a new chat","page":133,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use when: the chat is long, slow or confused, or you switch AI tools."},{"t":"prompt","text":"We are moving to a new chat. Update PROJECT_LOG.md and TODO_NEXT.md completely. Then write a HANDOFF note (under one page) covering: what the app is, current stage, what was done today, what is half-finished, known problems, rules that must not be broken, and the exact next step. Make a save point. I will paste the HANDOFF note into the new chat."},{"t":"p","text":"In the new chat, paste: “Read CLAUDE.md and the project files, then read this handoff note: [PASTE]. Summarise in 5 lines before doing anything.”"}]},{"id":"19.11","title":"The hostile auditor (use in a FRESH chat)","page":134,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use when: before every release, every 3 months, and after any big change. Open a brand-new chat that has never seen your building chats. Give it the project and nothing else."},{"t":"label","text":"HOSTILE AUDITOR PROMPT"},{"t":"p","text":"You are a hostile, independent auditor. You did not build this app and you do not trust anyone who did. Your job is to find what is wrong, missing, faked or risky. Assume previous claims are false until you see proof in the code, settings or test results. Check: (1) secrets in code, history or the built app; (2) database access rules for every table and storage bucket, using user A vs user B; (3) server-side checks for prices, rewards, roles and limits; (4) admin dashboard key handling, MFA and audit log; (5) tests that are skipped, weakened or that check nothing; (6) abuse limits on every costly action; (7) privacy policy and Data safety claims versus what the code really collects; (8) release build settings; (9) backups and restore steps; (10) edge cases around money and double taps. For each finding give: severity (critical / high / medium / low), exact proof (file and line, setting, or test), real-world impact in plain English, and the fix. Do not fix anything. Do not be polite. End with: “Would I let real users and real money touch this app today? Yes or no, and why.”"},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"Prompts guide the AI; they do not guarantee its behaviour. The proof checks in each chapter are what keep you safe. If a prompt’s answer has no proof, treat it as unverified."},{"t":"p","text":"➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Copy 19.3, 19.4 and 19.10 into your notes app; you will use them daily.","Run 19.5 on any app you have already built, then go to Chapter 20."]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"20","title":"Rescue: “I already built the app and it is insecure, slow or laggy”","page":135,"sections":[{"id":"20","title":"Introduction","page":135,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The exact order to fix things. Stop the bleeding first, then repair, then polish."},{"t":"p","text":"The uncomfortable truth first: if your app is live with real users and you have not checked its database rules or secret keys, assume it may already be exposed until proven otherwise. Do not add new features until Steps 1 to 4 are done. Speed comes after safety."},{"t":"label","text":"THE ORDER MATTERS"},{"t":"p","text":"Most people start with what annoys them most (speed, design). That is the wrong order. A fast app that leaks data is still a disaster. Follow these steps in order. Do not skip ahead."}]},{"id":"20-1","title":"Step 0: Freeze","page":135,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: Every new change on top of an unknown, unsafe base makes the mess bigger."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Stop all feature work. From now on, we only make changes from my rescue plan. Do not change anything yet. Confirm you understand."},{"t":"check","items":["No new features until Step 4 is done.","I told any helper or freelancer about the freeze."]}]},{"id":"20-2","title":"Step 1: Make sure you own and have saved everything","page":135,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: If you cannot access your code, keys or accounts, you cannot fix anything."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Check and tell me: is all the code in a private GitHub repository that I own? Are there uncommitted changes? Who has access to GitHub, Supabase, Play Console, payment and SMS accounts? Where is the upload key, and is it backed up? Make a save point of the current state and tag it “before-rescue”."},{"t":"check","items":["Code is in MY private GitHub, tagged “before-rescue”.","I am the owner of every account, with 2-step verification on.","Upload key and password are in my password manager plus one offline backup.","Production database has a fresh backup (or I upgraded to a plan with backups)."]}]},{"id":"20-3","title":"Step 2: Stop the bleeding (emergency checks)","page":136,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: These are the problems that can hurt you tonight."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Check only these, in order, and report with proof:\n1) Any secret, service-role, payment, SMS or AI key in the app, the admin website, or the Git history.\n2) Any table or storage bucket without RLS, or with rules that let any user read others’ data (run the Supabase Security Advisor and the user A vs user B test).\n3) Spend caps and billing alerts on every paid service.\n4) OTP and sign-up limits. Do not fix yet. Rank what you found by danger."},{"t":"check","items":["Any leaked key was ROTATED in the service’s dashboard (new key made, old key stopped).","Every table and bucket has RLS, and A-vs-B tests pass.","Spend caps and alerts are on everywhere.","OTP and sign-up limits exist.","If personal data was exposed: I followed the breach plan in Chapter 13."]}]},{"id":"20-4","title":"Step 3: Understand what you have","page":136,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: You cannot fix what nobody understands. A fresh map also gives future AIs the memory they lack."},{"t":"prompt","text":"In a FRESH chat: read the whole project without changing anything. Create or update CLAUDE.md, PROJECT_BRIEF.md (what the app does today), ARCHITECTURE.md, DATA_MAP.md, ACCESS_MATRIX.md and DEPENDENCIES.md, describing the app AS IT IS, including the messy parts. List everything you could not understand."},{"t":"check","items":["The project files describe the app as it really is today.","I read them and they match what I know.","A list of “unclear parts” exists."]}]},{"id":"20-5","title":"Step 4: Fix security properly","page":137,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: Step 2 stopped the worst. Now close the rest, in order of damage."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Run the hostile auditor prompt (Chapter 19) in a fresh chat. Then fix findings one at a time, critical first, each with a test and a save point. Keep a table in AUDIT_REPORT_[date].md: finding, severity, fix, test name, status."},{"t":"check","items":["All critical and high findings fixed, each with a test.","The auditor re-checked and agreed.","Payments, if any, are confirmed by server webhook only (Chapter 8)."]}]},{"id":"20-6","title":"Step 5: Protect the data for the future","page":137,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: Now that the data is locked, make sure it cannot be lost."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Set up and prove: automatic backups on production, a practice restore into a separate project, account deletion (in-app and web), and safe migrations for all future database changes. Update RUNBOOK.md."},{"t":"check","items":["Practice restore done and timed.","Account deletion works both ways.","RUNBOOK.md has restore steps."]}]},{"id":"20-7","title":"Step 6: Fix crashes and freezes","page":137,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: Crashes lose users and Play visibility faster than slowness."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Add crash reporting if missing. Using Play Console Android vitals and the crash dashboard, list crashes and ANRs ranked by users affected. Fix the top one, add a test, release with staged rollout, and repeat."},{"t":"check","items":["Crash reporting is live (test crash received).","Crash and ANR rates are below Google’s bad-behaviour lines (Chapter 10)."]}]},{"id":"20-8","title":"Step 7: Fix speed and lag","page":138,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: Now that it is safe and stable, make it pleasant."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Follow Chapter 10 exactly: measure first on a low-end phone and slow network, find causes with evidence, fix one at a time, before-and- after numbers in SPEED_REPORT.md."},{"t":"check","items":["Before-and-after table exists.","Startup and scrolling feel good on a cheap phone."]}]},{"id":"20-9","title":"Step 8: Fix edge cases, money first","page":138,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: These cause the angriest reviews."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Run the edge-case sweep (Chapter 11), starting with Group B (double taps) and Group J (money), then Group A (network) and Group C (app killed)."},{"t":"check","items":["5 fast taps never create duplicates.","Payment edge cases tested.","No blank screens offline or on first use."]}]},{"id":"20-10","title":"Step 9: Add the safety nets","page":138,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: So the app does not slide back into the same mess."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Set up CI with all tests, Dependabot alerts, secret scanning, kill switches, force-update, alerts to my phone and the Health page (Chapters 7, 9 and 16)."},{"t":"check","items":["CI green with 0 skipped.","Kill switch and force-update tested.","Alerts tested."]}]},{"id":"20-11","title":"Step 10: Check privacy and Play policy","page":139,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: Fixes may have changed what data you handle."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Re-check DATA_MAP.md, privacy policy, Data safety form and permissions against the app as it is now (Chapter 13). List anything that does not match."},{"t":"check","items":["Policy and Data safety match the real app.","Unneeded permissions removed."]}]},{"id":"20-12","title":"Step 11: Release the rescued version safely","page":139,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why now: A rushed release can undo the rescue."},{"t":"prompt","text":"Prepare the release (Chapter 15): release build checklist, internal test, my 20-minute phone test, then staged rollout with gates. If old versions are dangerous, set the minimum version to force users to update."},{"t":"check","items":["Staged rollout reached 100% with clean gates.","Old unsafe versions forced to update, if needed.","PROJECT_LOG.md has a “Rescue complete” entry with the date."]},{"t":"p","text":"Repair or rebuild?"},{"t":"p","text":"Sometimes rebuilding is cheaper than repairing. Ask a fresh AI to assess, and consider a rebuild if most of these are true:"},{"t":"check","items":["Nobody, including the AI, can explain how the main parts work.","Data is stored in a way that cannot be locked with proper rules without redesigning it.","Almost every fix breaks something else.","There are no tests and the code is very large for what the app does.","The app has few users, so moving them is easy."]},{"t":"prompt","text":"Assess this project honestly: should we repair it or rebuild it? Compare effort, risk and cost for both, list what could be reused (designs, database, text), and what a rebuild would need. Do not be optimistic."},{"t":"label","text":"HONEST LIMIT"},{"t":"p","text":"A rescue can make an app much safer, but it cannot undo a leak that already happened. If you suspect real user data was exposed, follow the breach plan and get legal advice. Also, for apps with money or children’s data, a human security review after the rescue is strongly recommended. ➤ WHAT YOU DO NEXT"},{"t":"check","items":["Do Steps 0 to 2 today, before anything else.","Book time this week for Steps 3 and 4.","Tick each step in PROJECT_LOG.md with the date as you finish it."]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"21","title":"Glossary","page":141,"sections":[{"id":"21","title":"Glossary","page":141,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every technical word in this book, in plain language."},{"t":"dl","items":[["AAB (Android App Bundle)","The file format you upload to Google Play. Google makes the right install file for each phone from it."],["Access matrix","A grid of who may read, add, change or delete each kind of data (Ch 6)."],["Accessibility","Making the app usable for everyone, including people with weak eyesight, shaky hands or who use screen readers."],["Admin dashboard","Your private website for managing the live app (Ch 14)."],["ANR","“App Not Responding”: the app freezes and Android offers to close it."],["Android vitals","Google’s real-world quality numbers for your app, inside Play Console."],["API","A doorway through which one program asks another for data or actions."],["API level / target API","The Android version your app is built for. Google Play requires a recent one every year."],["Audit log","A permanent record of every admin action."],["Authentication","Checking WHO someone is (login)."],["Authorization","Checking WHAT someone is allowed to do."],["Backend","The server side: database, files, sign-in and server code."],["Backup / restore","A saved copy of data, and bringing it back."],["Baseline Profile","A Google tool that makes app startup and scrolling faster."],["Bucket","A folder for files in Supabase Storage, with its own access rules."],["Cache","A saved copy kept nearby so things load faster."],["CAPTCHA","A check that a real human, not a bot, is using the form."],["CI (continuous integration)","A robot that builds the app and runs all tests on every save point."],["Cleartext","Unencrypted data sent over the internet. Must be blocked."],["Closed testing","Releasing to invited testers through Play Console before everyone."],["Cold start","Opening the app when it is not already running in memory."],["Commit / save point","A saved version of the project in Git."],["Compose (Jetpack Compose)","Google’s modern way to build Android screens in Kotlin."],["Consent","A user’s clear permission to use their data for a stated purpose."],["Crash rate","Share of daily users who saw the app crash."],["Data safety form","Play Console form declaring what data your app collects and shares."],["Debug / release build","Test version vs the locked-down version users get."],["Deep link","A link that opens a specific screen inside your app."],["Dependency / library","Ready-made code from others used inside your app."],["Dependabot","A GitHub tool that warns about libraries with known security problems."],["DPDP Act","India’s Digital Personal Data Protection law (Ch 13)."],["Edge case","Any situation that is not the normal, happy path."],["Edge Function","Small server code on Supabase for secret or trusted jobs."],["Encryption","Scrambling data so only those with the key can read it."],["Environment (dev / staging / prod)","Separate copies of the backend for experiments, rehearsal and real users."],["Feature flag / kill switch","A remote setting to turn a feature on or off without a new app version."],["Force update","Making users of an old version update before continuing."],["Git / GitHub","Version control (save points) / the website that stores them online."],["HTTPS","Encrypted internet connection."],["Idempotent","Safe to repeat: doing it twice has the same effect as once."],["Jank","Stuttering when scrolling or animating."],["Key (API key, secret key)","A long password that lets a program use a service."],["Key rotation","Making a new key and switching off the old one."],["Kotlin","The main programming language for Android apps."],["Lazy list","A list that only draws the items you can see."],["Load test","Simulating many users at once to find limits."],["Lock-in","How hard it is to move away from a provider."],["MASVS","OWASP’s professional checklist for mobile app security (8 groups)."],["Material Design 3","Google’s design system for Android apps."],["MCP","A standard for connecting AI tools to outside services (Ch 18)."],["MFA / 2-step verification","Logging in with a password plus a second proof, like a code app."],["Migration","A saved script that changes the database structure safely."],["MVP","Minimum Viable Product: the smallest first version that solves the main problem."],["Obfuscation (R8)","Making the app’s code smaller and harder to read."],["OTP","One-time password, usually a code sent by SMS or email."],["OWASP","A non-profit that publishes free security standards."],["Permission (Android)","Phone access an app asks for: camera, location, etc."],["PITR","Point-in-Time Recovery: restoring the database to a specific moment."],["Play App Signing","Google keeps your app signing key; you keep an upload key."],["Play Integrity API","Google check that a request comes from your real app on a real phone."],["Plugin","A bundle of skills, connectors and settings for an AI tool."],["Pre-launch report","Google’s automatic test of your app on real devices in Play Console."],["Privacy policy","A public page explaining what data you collect and why."],["Production","The real, live system with real users."],["Prompt injection","Hidden instructions inside data that trick an AI."],["Rate limit","A cap on how often an action can be repeated."],["Red team","Attacking your own app on purpose to find weaknesses."],["Repository (repo)","The project’s folder of code and history on GitHub."],["RLS (Row Level Security)","Database rules that check access for every single row."],["Rollback","Going back to the last good version."],["Runbook","Step-by-step instructions for emergencies and routine jobs."],["SDK","A kit of code from a company (e.g. a payment SDK) used in your app."],["Service-role / secret key","A Supabase key that ignores all access rules. Server only."],["Session / token","The temporary pass that keeps a user logged in."],["Skill","A saved instruction file for a kind of AI task."],["Spend cap","A hard limit on how much a service can charge you."],["Staged rollout","Releasing an update to a percentage of users first."],["Staging","A rehearsal copy of production for final checks."],["Supabase","The backend service this book uses: database, sign-in, files, server functions."],["TalkBack","Android’s screen reader for blind and low-vision users."],["Threat model","A written list of what attackers want, how they would try, and what stops them."],["Unit / integration / UI test","Tests of one piece / pieces together / screens and taps."],["Upload key","Your key for sending new versions to Google Play."],["Webhook","A message one service sends to your server when something happens (e.g. payment confirmed)."],["Wireframe","A rough black-and-white drawing of a screen."]]}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"22","title":"Master checklist and “before launch” page","page":145,"sections":[{"id":"22","title":"Introduction","page":145,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"One page for the whole journey. One page you never skip before launch."},{"t":"p","text":"Master checklist (whole journey)"},{"t":"check","items":["Stage: done when","Idea: 5 real users heard; promise written (Ch 3)","Requirements: MUST list, user stories, “not now” list in PROJECT_BRIEF.md (Ch 3)","Costs: cost table with links; budget limit set (Ch 3)","Design: flows, wireframes approved; states grid ticked; accessibility checked (Ch 4)","Architecture: ARCHITECTURE.md; dev and prod separate; spend caps on (Ch 5)","Database: DATA_MAP + ACCESS_MATRIX; RLS + A-vs-B tests; restore tested (Ch 6)","Build: private GitHub; save points; CI green; no secrets (Ch 7)","Security: threat model; 8.2–8.11 done with proof; self-audit clean (Ch 8)","Abuse: ABUSE_LIMITS.md tested; Play Integrity; kill switch + force-update (Ch 9)","Speed: before-and-after numbers on a cheap phone (Ch 10)","Edge cases: EDGE_CASES.md handled and tested (Ch 11)","Testing: all levels; red-team; fresh-AI audit highs fixed (Ch 12)","Privacy: policy live; consent; deletion both ways; Data safety true (Ch 13)","Admin: no secret key in browser; MFA; roles; audit log (Ch 14)","Release: closed test; signing backed up; staged rollout (Ch 15)","Live: crash reports; alerts; Health page; incident plan (Ch 16)","Healthy: routines in calendar (Ch 17)"]},{"t":"h","text":"BEFORE LAUNCH — do not skip a single line"},{"t":"p","text":"App: ______________________ Version: ________ Date: ____________"},{"t":"check","items":["Check (with proof seen by me)","Release build: not debuggable, R8 on, no DEV label, points to production","Search of BUILT app and admin site: no secret keys","Every table and bucket has RLS; A-vs-B and change-the-ID tests pass","Prices, rewards, roles and limits enforced on the server","Payments confirmed only by verified webhook; double-confirmation safe","Rate limits tested: OTP, sign-up, promos, uploads, AI calls","Spend caps and billing alerts on for every paid service","Production on a plan with backups; practice restore done","Kill switch and force-update tested on my phone","Crash reporting live; alerts reach my phone","CI green, 0 skipped tests; fresh-AI audit has no critical/high open","My 20-minute real-phone test passed (Ch 12.8)","5 fast taps create no duplicates; offline and first-use screens OK","Privacy policy and terms live; link in Play Console and in app","Account deletion works in app AND on web page","Data safety form matches the app and all libraries","Content rating, target audience, ads forms done; age policy decided","Store listing honest; screenshots from the real build","Upload key + password in password manager AND offline backup","2-step verification on Play Console, Supabase, GitHub, hosting, payments","Closed test requirement met; pre-launch report clean","Incident plan and rollback plan written in RUNBOOK.md","Human expert review done if money, health or children (Ch 8, 13)"]},{"t":"p","text":"Signed: ____________________ All boxes ticked? If not, do not launch."},{"t":"h","text":"Sources, with the date each was read"},{"t":"p","text":"⏰ All read on 22 November 2026. Facts marked CHECK THE DATE must be re-checked"},{"t":"p","text":"on these pages before each launch."},{"t":"pre","text":"Source                              Where                                         Ch. Read\nGoogle Play: Target API level       support.google.com/googleplay/                2, 17 22 Nov\nrequirements\n                                    android-developer/answer/11926878                   2026"},{"t":"p","text":"Android Developers: Meet Play’s target developer.android.com/google/play/ 2, 17 22 Nov"},{"t":"pre","text":"API level requirement               requirements/target-sdk                             2026\nPIB: DPDP Rules, 2025 notified      pib.gov.in (17 Nov 2025 release)              2, 13 22 Nov\n                                                                                             2026\nDPDP Rules 2025 law-firm summaries  mondaq.com; azbpartners.com;                  2, 13 22 Nov\n(phases, 72-hour breach report,     amsshardul.com; india-briefing.com                       2026\nparental consent)\nSupabase: Pricing and Billing FAQ   supabase.com/pricing;                         3, 5  22 Nov\n(spend cap)                                                                             2026\n                                    supabase.com/docs/guides/platform/"},{"t":"p","text":"billing-faq"},{"t":"pre","text":"Supabase: Project pausing (Free plan) supabase.com/docs/guides/                   5     22 Nov\n                                                        platform/project-pausing\n                                                                                        2026\nSupabase: Clerk third-party auth    supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/                5     22 Nov\n                                                                                        2026\n                                    third-party/clerk\nClerk: Android SDK general availability clerk.com/changelog/2025-09-11-           5     22 Nov\n                                                                                        2026\n                                    android-sdk-ga\nFirebase: Pricing plans; Cloud Storage firebase.google.com/docs/projects/ 5             22 Nov\n                                                                                        2026\nplan change FAQ                     billing/firebase-pricing-plans\nSupabase: Phone login (SMS          supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/                5, 9 22 Nov\nproviders, India TRAI DLT note)     phone-login                                              2026\nAndroid Developers: Guide to app    developer.android.com/topic/                  5     22 Nov\narchitecture                        architecture\n                                                                                        2026\nAndroid Developers: Make apps more developer.android.com/guide/                   4     22 Nov\naccessible                          topics/ui/accessibility/apps                        2026\nAndroid Accessibility Help: Touch   support.google.com/accessibility/             4     22 Nov\ntarget size                                                                             2026\n                                    android/answer/7101858\nSupabase: Row Level Security        supabase.com/docs/guides/                     6, 8  22 Nov\n                                                                                        2026\n                                    database/postgres/row-level-security\nSupabase: Production security       supabase.com/docs/guides/                     6, 8 22 Nov\nchecklist; Security Advisor         security/production-checklist                            2026\nSource                                Where                              Ch. Read\nSupabase: Database backups and        supabase.com/docs/guides/          6      22 Nov\nPITR                                  platform/backups\n                                                                                2026\nOWASP MASVS v2.1.0                    mas.owasp.org                      8      22 Nov"},{"t":"p","text":"2026"},{"t":"pre","text":"OWASP Mobile Top 10 (2024)            owasp.org/www-project-mobile-top- 8       22 Nov\n                                      10                                        2026\nSupabase: Auth rate limits            supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/     9      22 Nov\n                                                                                2026\n                                      rate-limits\nSupabase: Enable CAPTCHA              supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/     9      22 Nov\nprotection                                                                      2026\n                                      auth-captcha"},{"t":"p","text":"Android Developers: Play Integrity API developer.android.com/google/play/ 8, 9  22 Nov 2026"},{"t":"pre","text":"overview and setup                    integrity\nAndroid Developers: Android vitals    developer.android.com/topic/       10     22 Nov\n(core vitals thresholds)              performance/vitals\n                                                                                2026\nAndroid Developers: App startup time; developer.android.com/topic/       10     22 Nov\nMeasuring performance                 performance/launch-time                   2026\nGoogle Play: Account deletion         support.google.com/googleplay/     6, 13 22 Nov\nrequirements\n                                      android-developer/answer/13327111         2026\nGoogle Play: Data safety section      support.google.com/googleplay/     13     22 Nov\n                                                                                2026\n                                      android-developer/answer/10787469\nGoogle Play: User Data policy         support.google.com/googleplay/     13     22 Nov\n                                                                                2026\n                                      android-developer/answer/10144311\nSupabase: Multi-factor authentication; supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/    14     22 Nov\nMFA via RLS                           auth-mfa                                  2026\nGoogle Play: Testing requirements for support.google.com/googleplay/     15     22 Nov\n                                                                                2026\nnew personal accounts                 android-developer/answer/14151465\nGoogle Play: Use Play App Signing     support.google.com/googleplay/     15     22 Nov\n                                                                                2026\n                                      android-developer/answer/9842756\nGoogle Play: Staged rollouts; Halt a  support.google.com/googleplay/     15     22 Nov\nfully rolled-out release                                                        2026\n                                      android-developer/answer/6346149\nAndroid Developers: Android           developer.android.com/developer- 15       22 Nov\ndeveloper verification                verification                              2026\nSupabase: MCP server docs; Defense supabase.com/docs/guides/ai-          18     22 Nov\nin Depth for MCP                      tools/mcp                                 2026\nClaude Code docs: Extend Claude       code.claude.com/docs/en/features- 18      22 Nov\nCode                                  overview                                  2026"},{"t":"p","text":"Not verified from an official source and stated as guidance only: the budget-phone speed targets in Chapter 10, and the 5-second “excessive cold start” line, which comes from summaries of Android vitals rather than a Google page read directly. Prices are deliberately not printed; check each provider’s pricing page. This book is not legal, financial or professional security advice."}]}],"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book1.pdf"},{"id":"P","title":"Print Pack: sheets to fill in for every app","page":1,"pdf":"App_Builders_Handbook_Book2_Print_Pack.pdf","sections":[{"id":"P.1","title":"App info sheet","page":3,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"App: ______________________________ Date: ______________ One-sentence promise: For [who], it [does what], unlike [what they use now]"},{"t":"p","text":"Who the users are (age, phone types, internet) App type(s): shop / social / payments / kids / utility / AI / health-finance MUST features for version 1 (3–7)"},{"t":"p","text":"NOT in this version Notes from 5 real user talks"},{"t":"p","text":"Monthly budget limit (₹) and cost at 1,000 users Accounts: Play Console owner email / Supabase dev + prod names / GitHub repo Human expert needed? (money, health, children) — who and when"}]},{"id":"P.2","title":"Decisions log","page":4,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"App: ______________________________ Date: ______________ Page ____ Write every important decision. Copy into PROJECT_LOG.md."},{"t":"pre","text":"Date  Decision  Why                                  Options rejected"}]},{"id":"P.3","title":"Threat list","page":5,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"App: ______________________________ Date: ______________ From THREAT_MODEL.md (Ch 8.1). Top threats first."},{"t":"p","text":"# Who attacks What they want / how  Damage Lock that stops it  Tested"},{"t":"pre","text":"(H/M/L)                    ☐"}]},{"id":"P.4","title":"Security tick sheet","page":6,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"App: ______________________________ Date: ______________ Tick only with proof. Write the proof (test name, setting, screenshot)."},{"t":"check","items":["Lock (MASVS group)                                        Proof","No secrets in app, admin site or Git history (STORAGE/CRYPTO)","RLS on every table + A-vs-B tests (AUTH)","Storage buckets locked + A-vs-B test (AUTH)","Change-the-ID tests fail as they should (AUTH)","Normal user cannot do admin actions (AUTH)","Logout ends session on server; log out everywhere works (AUTH)","Re-confirm for sensitive actions (AUTH)","Cleartext blocked; no “trust all certificates” (NETWORK)","Phone storage table; no personal data in logs (STORAGE)","Exported components and deep links reviewed (PLATFORM)","Server validates all input; prices never trusted (CODE)","Dependabot alerts 0; DEPENDENCIES.md current (CODE)","Force-update works (CODE)","R8 on; Play Integrity checked on server (RESILIENCE)","Payments: server price + verified webhook (Ch 8.9)","Cost limits and spend caps (Ch 8.11)","Admin: MFA, roles, audit log, no secret key in browser (Ch 14)","Minimum data; consent; deletion (PRIVACY)"]}]},{"id":"P.5","title":"Speed targets and results","page":7,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"App: ______________________________ Date: ______________ Test phone: ______________ (RAM ____ GB) Network: ______________"},{"t":"pre","text":"Measure                      My target  Before  After             Date  Met ☐\nCold start to usable screen                                             ☐\nScrolling main list (slow    below                                      ☐\nframes)                      1.09%\nMemory use                   below                                      ☐\nApp download size            0.47%                                      ☐\nFirst useful screen on slow                                             ☐\nnetwork\nBattery (10-minute session)                                             ☐\nData used (10-minute                                                    ☐\nsession)\nCrash rate (Android vitals)                                             ☐\nANR rate (Android vitals)                                               ☐"},{"t":"p","text":"What changed (one fix per line)"}]},{"id":"P.6","title":"Edge-case tick sheet","page":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"App: ______________________________ Date: ______________ Do each test yourself on your phone (Ch 11). Write what happened if it failed."},{"t":"check","items":["Test                                                          Notes","A Airplane mode on every screen; drop internet mid-action","A Slow network; Wi-Fi with no internet","B Tap every important button 5 times fast","B Back button while loading; leave and return mid-form","C “Don’t keep activities” on; switch apps and return","C Phone call mid-task; rotate; battery saver; restart","D Deny each permission, deny forever, remove in Settings","E Wrong phone date; other time zone; midnight/month-end","F Same account on two phones; log out everywhere","F Deleted / blocked account; two people on one phone","G Old version after server change; update over old version","G Kill switch off; force-update on","H Empty, very long, emoji, Hindi, symbols; 0 / −1 / 99999","H Huge file; wrong file type; brand-new empty account","I Storage full; cheap phone; biggest font; dark mode; tablet","J Close app after paying; cancel payment; refund; price change","K Under-18 policy; TalkBack main task; undo / confirm"]}]},{"id":"P.7","title":"Pre-launch checklist","page":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"App: ______________________________ Date: ______________ Version: ________ Use Chapter 22’s “Before launch” page line by line, then sign here."},{"t":"check","items":["Chapter 22 “Before launch” page: every box ticked with proof","Hostile auditor (fresh chat) report: no critical or high open","My 20-minute real-phone test passed on the release build","Rollout plan and gates written; I know where “Halt” is","Incident plan ready; alerts tested this week"]},{"t":"p","text":"Open risks I am knowingly accepting (and why)"},{"t":"h","text":"Launch decision: GO / NO-GO Signed and dated"}]},{"id":"P.8","title":"Weekly post-launch check","page":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"App: ______________________________ Date: ______________ Week of: ____________"},{"t":"pre","text":"Check                                 Normal?        Notes / action"},{"t":"check","items":["Yes ☐ No"]},{"t":"h","text":"Health page: sign-ups, logins, main"},{"t":"pre","text":"actions                               ☐ Yes ☐ No\nCrash and ANR rates (below Google     ☐ None ☐ Some\nlines?)                               ☐ Yes ☐ No"},{"t":"check","items":["Yes ☐ No"]},{"t":"pre","text":"New crash types                       ☐ Yes ☐ No"},{"t":"check","items":["Done"]},{"t":"h","text":"Backend errors"},{"t":"check","items":["Done"]},{"t":"p","text":"OTPs, sign-ups, promo use (abuse?)"},{"t":"check","items":["Yes ☐ No"]},{"t":"pre","text":"Costs this week vs budget             ☐ N/A ☐ Done"},{"t":"p","text":"Reviews and support messages read and answered"},{"t":"p","text":"Repeated complaints turned into TODO + test"},{"t":"h","text":"Backups ran"},{"t":"h","text":"Rollout in progress? Gate checked"},{"t":"h","text":"Top 3 actions for next week"}]}]}]};
